[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2506.09609, 'Schramm-Loewner evolution contains a topological Sierpi\'nski carpet when $\kappa$ is clos'] The following holds almost surely. For any $w \in [-1/2,1/2]^2$, suppose that the following conditions are satisfied:
    \begin{enumerate}[(1)]
        \item The origin is $0$-good;
        \item For any $n \geq 1$ and $x \in \epsilon^n \mathbb{Z}^2$ with $w \in B(x, \epsilon^n)$, the point $x$ is $n$-good.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, $\eta^L(w) \cap \eta^R(w) = \{w \}$, namely, $w \in \mathcal{K}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

