[Lem lem of arXiv:2411.04802, 'Dynkin ghost games with asymmetry and consolation'] Let $\Gamma^1,\Gamma^2\in\mathcal A$, denote by $\gamma_1$ and
$\gamma_2$ the corresponding randomised stopping times, and let $\gamma_i(u)$, $i=1,2$ be defined as in (equation gammai of the paper). Then 
\begin{equation}
J_1(x;\gamma_1,\gamma_2)= \int_0^1 J_1(x;\gamma_1(u),\gamma_2)\,du
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
J_2(x;\gamma_1,\gamma_2)=\int_0^1 J_2(x;\gamma_1,\gamma_2(u))\,du.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Pro eq:pibar of arXiv:2411.07158, 'Markov chains on trees: almost lower and upper directed cases'] If ${\sf M}$ is the transition of an irreducible random walk on $T$, then the $h$-invariant measure of ${\sf M}$ is $\overline\pi=(\overline\pi_u,u\in T)$ defined by  $\overline{\pi}(\varnothing)=1$, and for $u\in T$,
\begin{eqnarray}
\overline{\pi}_u=\overline{\pi}(\varnothing)\prod_{j=1}^h\frac{ M_{u[j-1],u[j]}}{ M_{u[j],u[j-1]}},~~\textrm{ for } u\in T.
\end{eqnarray}
Therefore, ${\sf M}$ is positive recurrent, if and only if $\sum_{u \in T} \overline{\pi}_u<+\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition defincomplete of arXiv:2504.14457, 'Abstract'] The incomplete gamma function is defined as:
\begin{align}
    \gamma^{*}(\nu,z):=e^{-z}\sum_{m=0}^\infty \frac{z^m}{\Gamma(\nu+m+1)},
\end{align}
for all $z\in \mathbb{R}$ and $\nu>0$.

