body:
[Theorem th:main2 of arXiv:2601.07759, 'The value of random zero-sum games'] We fix $n\geq 2$. The value of the random game defined by 
\begin{equation*}
    v(Q) = \min_{{\boldsymbol x}\in \Delta_n}\max_{{\boldsymbol y}\in\Delta_n} {\boldsymbol x}^T Q {\boldsymbol y},
\end{equation*}
where $Q\in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$ is drawn from the Haar measure on orthogonal matrices, satisfies, for all $t>0$, 
\begin{equation}
    v(Q)\leq \frac{t}{n\sqrt{n}},
\end{equation}
with probability at least $1-c_1\exp(-c_2 t^2)-c_3\exp(-c_4 n)$, for universal constants $c_1,c_2,c_3,c_4>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma psi_exp of arXiv:2412.07258, 'Permuton limit of a generalization of the Mallows and $k$-card-minimum models'] For any $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$ and $T \in \mathbb{R}_+$ we have
    \begin{align}
   \lim_{\alpha \to \infty}
   \delta(\alpha, \varepsilon,T)=0, \quad \text{where} \quad
  \delta(\alpha, \varepsilon,T):=    \sup_{x \in [\varepsilon, 1-\varepsilon]} \sup_{y \in [-T,T]}
\left|
\frac{g_\alpha(x) e^{-\psi(x)\cdot y}}{g_\alpha\big(x+\frac{y}{\gamma(\alpha)}\big)}
 - 1 \right| =0.
    \end{align}

