[Definition 63 of arXiv:2608.29086, '2608.29086'] [$v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x)$]
Given $Cone\ A \in CONE(H_0)$. A one-to-one mapping
\begin{equation*}
v_{{\partial}Cone\ A} :\ H_0 \rightarrow {\partial}Cone\ A
\end{equation*}
is uniquely defined by the equation
\begin{equation*}
{\partial}Cone\ A \cap \pi^{-1}(x) = \{ v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x) \}.
\end{equation*}
In particular, ${\pi}(v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x))=x$. $v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x)$ is called the \textit{surface map} of $Cone\ A$. In the following, \underline{we often identify $Cone\ A$ with $v_{{\partial}Cone\ A}(x)$}.

[Theorem th:MLSI of arXiv:2511.05223, 'Kac's Program and Relative Entropy Decay for Nonlinear Spin-Exchange Dynamics'] Let \(J\in{\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} ^{n\times n}\) be a nonnegative definite matrix with largest eigenvalue \(\lambda(J) < 1/2\), and let  \(\overline{J}=\max_{i\in [n]} \sum_{j\in [n]}|J_{i,j}|\). 
Then, 
for any partition $\ensuremath{\mathcal A}$ of $[n]$, for any $N\ge 2$, and $\r\in \ensuremath{\mathcal D}_{\ensuremath{\mathcal A},N}$, the Markov chain with Dirichlet form (equation Dirichlet form of the paper) defined by $J$ and $\ensuremath{\mathcal K}=\ensuremath{\mathcal K}_\ensuremath{\mathcal A}$ given in (equation eq:meanf0 of the paper),  satisfies 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}_{N, J,\r}(F, \log F) \ge \a\operatorname{Ent}_{\mu_{N,J,\r}}F,
\end{equation}
for all $F\ge 0$, with constant  $\a$ such that  
  \begin{equation}
        \alpha \ge \tfrac1{4n}(1 - 2\lambda(J))^2\,e^{-16\overline{J}}\,.

   \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 15 of arXiv:2604.00882, 'On some extensions of generalized counting processes'] The probabilities ${\tilde p}_n^\nu(t)$
satisfy the following  equation
\begin{equation}
	\sum_{j=1}^N \,\mu_j \frac{d^{\nu_j} }{dt^{\nu_j}}\, \tilde p_n^\nu = - \Lambda \tilde p_n(t) + \sum_{j=1}^{\min\{n,k\}}\lambda_j \tilde p_{n-j}(t), \,\, n\geq0,
\end{equation}
with the standard initial condition and the  fractional derivatives in the C-D sense.

If  \eqref{eq:T12} holds, then 
	the probabilities ${\tilde p}_n^\nu(t)$,  $n = 0, 1, \dots$, $t \ge 0$, can be given as follows:
		\begin{align*}
		\tilde p_n^\nu(t) &= \sum_{r=0}^n \sum_{\substack{\alpha_1+\alpha_2+\dots+\alpha_k=r \\ \alpha_1+2\alpha_2+\dots+k\alpha_k=n}} \binom{r}{\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \dots, \alpha_k} \lambda_1^{\alpha_1} \lambda_2^{\alpha_2} \dots \lambda_k^{\alpha_k} \ p_r^\nu(t),
	\end{align*}
	where $p_k^\nu(t)$ are defined in \eqref{eq:T12_2}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition epsilon_2 of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] Let $\epsilon_2(\kappa_N,z)$ be the function of $\zeta$ in (equation w2 of the paper), then
      \begin{equation}
        |\epsilon_2(\kappa_N,z)|\le \frac{\mathbf{M}(\kappa_N^{2/3}\zeta)}{\mathbf{E}(\kappa_N^{2/3}\zeta)}\left[\exp\left(\frac{\lambda_0 \mathcal{V}(\zeta)}{\kappa_N}\right)-1\right].
    \end{equation}
The function $\mathcal{V}$ is defined as $\mathcal{V}(\zeta):=\int_\zeta^\infty|\Psi(t)|t|^{-\frac{1}{2}}dt$ and the constant $\lambda_0:=\sup\limits_{x\in \mathbb{R}}\left\{\pi|x|^\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{M}^2(x)\right\}<\infty$.

