[Lemma lemm:lawHolcombi of arXiv:2607.24640, 'The Yang-Mills measure on surfaces via Morse theory'] The law of $\mathbf{Hol}_{r}   \stackrel{ \mathcal{L} }{=}  \mathrm{b}^{\varepsilon_{4g}}_{a(t_4g)} \dots \mathrm{b}^{\varepsilon_1}_{a(t_1)} \mathrm{g}_r(t=1) $ (see Lemma~(lemma:BM_with_jumps of the paper)) is given by the formula
\begin{align*}
     \mathbb{P} _{\operatorname{YM}}^{{\rm free}}\left( \mathbf{Hol}_{r} \in d\mathrm{h} \right)
= & \  \mathbb{P} _{\operatorname{YM}}^{{\rm free}}\left(\mathrm{b}^{\varepsilon_{4g}}_{a(t_4g)} \dots \mathrm{b}^{\varepsilon_1}_{a(t_1)}\mathrm{g}_r(1)  \in d \mathrm{h} \right) \\
= &  \sum_{\rho\in \widehat{G} } e^{-{\frac{c_2(\rho)}{2}\mathscr{A}_r(1) }}    
      \frac{ \chi_\rho(\mathrm{h})}{\dim(V_{\rho})^{2g-1}}\, \mu_G(d\mathrm{h})=: p_{ {\bf Hol},r}(\mathrm{h}) \mu_G(d \mathrm{h}),
\end{align*}
where $\mathscr{A}_r(1):=\mathscr{A}_{\gamma_r}(1)$ for $r>0$ and $\mathscr{A}_0(1)=\upsilon(\Sigma)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary 23 (Average 2) of arXiv:2605.02499, '2605.02499'] Let $\gs_0 \subseteq [N]$ and let $S= R_{\gs_0}$ be a set of type
configurations of the lines in the sample $\gs_0$. Let $\overline{\Phi}(S, \bs{w}, \sP^-)$ be the Frankenstein process on paths from Def.~\ref{def.Frankenstein_path} and $\Phi(S)$ be the Frankenstein process from Def.~\ref{def.Frankenstein}. Let $M_t$ denote the number of
interactive neutral events in the quasi-AIG $\As^{-}_{[0,t]}(\gs_0)$ and let $\Gamma^{(i)}\in \{r,b\}^N$ chosen uniformly across all configurations with $i$ entries equal to $\mathrm{r}$ and $N-i$ entries equal to $\mathrm{b}$. Then, for any $t \ge 0$, we have 
\begin{equation}
     \Eb \Big[\frac{1}{2^{M_t}} \sum_{\bs w\in \{\ddownarrow,\uuparrow\}^{M_t}}  \Pb\big(\Gamma^{(i)}\in \overline{\Phi}_t(S,\bs w, \sP^-)\mid \sP^-\big)\Big]= \Pb\big(\Gamma^{(i)}\in \Phi_t(S)\big).
\end{equation}

[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2506.15159, 'Conditional central limit theorems for exponential random graphs'] Consider the ERGM \cref{eq:ERGM} with parameters in the subset $B$ of the subcritical region considered in \cref{thm:twostar}.
   For any graph $K$, if $v(K)=3$, we have
 \begin{align*}
     \IE\widetilde{K}=O(n^2).
 \end{align*}
 Moreover, if $v(K)\geq 4$, we have
\begin{align*}
     \IE\widetilde{K}=O(n^{v-2}).
 \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop 1 (Pointwise Density estimates for the driving noise) of arXiv:2604.20323, '2604.20323'] There exists $C\ge 1,\bar c\ge 1$ s.t. for all $0\textcolor{black}{\le} s<t\le T $, $x,w \in \R^d$, and any muti-index $\ba \in \N^d $, $|\ba|\in \{0,1, 2\} $,  
        $\theta\in \{0,1\} $,
    \begin{align}
    |\partial_t^\theta\partial_x^\ba p_\alpha (t-s,w-x)|&\le C (t-s)^{-(\theta+\frac{|\ba|+d}{\alpha})}\left(1+\frac{\left| w-x\right|}{(t-s)^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}} \right)^{-(d+\alpha+|\ba|)},\ \alpha\in (1,2),\notag\\
        |\partial_t^\theta\partial_x^\ba p_2(t-s,w-x)|&\le C(t-s)^{-(\theta+\frac{|\ba|+d}{\alpha})} \bar p_2(t-s,w-x). 
    \end{align}
 In particular, for $|\ba|\in \{1,2\} $ (i.e. when spatial derivatives are considered) and $\rho\in (0,1) $ 
     \begin{align}
        |w-y|^\rho|\partial_t^\theta\partial_{\textcolor{black}{w}}^\ba  p_\alpha(t-s,y-w)|\le C (t-s)^{-\theta+\frac{\rho-|\ba|}{\alpha}} \bar p_\alpha(t-s,y-w).
    \end{align}
    and, for $y\in \R^d$ s.t. $|y-y'|\lesssim (t-s)^{\frac{1}{\alpha}}$, 
    \begin{align}
    	|w-y|^\rho|\partial_t^\theta\partial_w^\ba p_\alpha\left(t-s,{y-w+(y'-y)}\right)|\le C (t-s)^{-\theta+\frac{\rho-|\ba|}{\alpha}} \bar p_\alpha(t-s,y-w).
    \end{align}
	\color{black} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 68 (Logarithmic tensor calculus and quasi-Banach sewing) of arXiv:2608.08756, 'Infinite-Dimensional L\'evy Area and Spectral Ancestry'] For fixed integers \(r,s\geq1\),

\begin{align}
 \|A\otimes B\|_{\mathfrak W^{[r+s]}}
 &\leq C_{r,s}
 \|A\|_{\mathfrak W^{[r]}}
 \|B\|_{\mathfrak W^{[s]}},
 \\
 \|A\otimes C\|_{\mathfrak W^{[r]}}
 &\leq C_r
 \|A\|_{\mathfrak W^{[r]}}\|C\|_{\mathcal S_1}.

\end{align}

If one positive-grade factor is little, the corresponding output is little.
The same assertions hold flattening by flattening for concrete tensor
products in every fixed tensor degree.  First-order Hilbert vectors and
finite-rank factors have zero logarithmic cost.

Let \(B_{s,u,t}\) be a closed three-increment with values in
\(\mathfrak W_{\mathrm{mf}}^{[r]}(H^{\otimes n})\), and suppose

\[
 \|B_{s,u,t}\|_{\mathfrak W_{\mathrm{mf}}^{[r]}(n)}
 \leq C\omega(s,t)^\gamma,
 \qquad \gamma>1,
\]

for a continuous control \(\omega\).  There is a unique two-increment
\(\Lambda B\) with \(\delta\Lambda B=B\) and

\begin{equation}
 \|\Lambda B_{s,t}\|_{\mathfrak W_{\mathrm{mf}}^{[r]}(n)}
 \leq C_{n,r,\gamma}C\omega(s,t)^\gamma.

\end{equation}

If all defects are little, every sewn increment is little.

[Definition def:sol_gen_MKV of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] A (general) solution to MKV$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma})$ is a triple $( W, (\mathcal{F}_t)_t, X)$ which is a (general) solution to SDE$({\bf b}_{\boldsymbol\mu},{\boldsymbol\sigma}_{\boldsymbol\mu};0)$ (see Definition (def:sol_gen_SDE of the paper)), where ${\bf b}_{\boldsymbol\mu},{\boldsymbol\sigma}_{\boldsymbol\mu}$ are as in (equation eq:def_frozen_coeff of the paper) with
\begin{equation}
{\boldsymbol\mu}_t  = [X_t], \qquad t\geq 0.\vspace{-10pt}
\end{equation}

[Corollary 54 (Image measure theorem) of arXiv:2604.01685, 'Probability with Measure\footnote{Lecture notes. University of Ljubljana.}'] Let $(\Omega,\FF,\mu)$ be a measure space, $(\Omega',\FF')$ a measurable space and $f\in \FF/\FF'$. Then $f_\star \mu$ is a measure on $\FF'$, finite or probability accordingly as  $\mu$ is so. If further $g\in \FF'/\mathcal{B}_{[-\infty\infty]}$, then 
	\begin{equation}
	\int g\dd(f_\star\mu)=\int g\circ f\dd\mu,
	\end{equation}
	the integral on the left-hand side being well-defined iff 	the integral on the right-hand side is well-defined.

