[Definition Harmonicity of arXiv:2511.08956, '\textbf{Towards a characterization of elliptic Harnack inequality for jump processes}'] For all open $D \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$, we say that a function $h:\mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ is \emph{harmonic} on $D$ (with respect to $X$) if for all open $U \Subset D$ and for all $x$, we have
    \begin{equation*}
        h(x) = \mathbb{E}_x \left[ h(X_{\tau_U}) \right].
    \end{equation*}
    (Note that when $X$ is a jump process, the harmonicity of $h$ on $D$ depends on the values $h$ takes on $D^c$, because for $U \Subset D$, $\tau_U$ need not be in $D$.)

