[Corollary cor:optimal of arXiv:2606.24465, 'History estimation in random recursive trees:\\Pointwise approach via iterated Jordan cent'] For each $\alpha>0$, there exists a constant $c_\alpha$ such that for any label-invariant estimator $\widehat{\sigma}$, and any $n\ge 2$,
    \begin{equation*}
        R_{\alpha}(\widehat{\sigma}) \ge 
        \begin{dcases}
            c_\alpha n^{2-\alpha}, &\text{if }\alpha\in(0,2),\\
            c_\alpha\log n,&\text{if }\alpha=2, \\
            c_\alpha,&\text{if }\alpha>2.
        \end{dcases}
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:invariance of arXiv:2605.03896, 'Dimer models on astroidal zig-zag graphs'] The formula (equation eq:formula_inverse_K of the paper) is:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Invariant under swapping $\mathsf x$ and $\mathsf y$.
\item Invariant under fixing $I_\mathsf w$ and expanding $I_\mathsf b$ across any edge $e$ 
such that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\operatorname{sign}_{{\mathsf{R}}_\mathsf b}(e)=+$, or
    \item $\mathsf b$ lies on $\beta_e$ and $e$ is black (in particular, this holds whenever $\mathsf b$ is $\beta_e$-outer).
\end{itemize}
Precisely, if $I_\mathsf b' = [\ell_{\mathsf b}',r_\mathsf b']$ is obtained from $I_\mathsf b$ by a sequence of such expansions, then
\begin{equation}
\mathsf K^{-1}_{\mathsf b,\mathsf w}
=\operatorname{sign}(v)\left(
\iint_{C(I_{\mathsf w}) \prec C(I_{\mathsf b}')} \omega_{\mathsf b,\mathsf w}
-
\langle \partial I_\mathsf b' \cap I_\mathsf w,\mathsf A_{\mathsf b,\mathsf w} \rangle
\right).
\end{equation}
Similarly, we can fix $I_\mathsf b$ and expand $I_\mathsf w$ across any edge such that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\operatorname{sign}_{{\mathsf{R}}_\mathsf w}(e)=-$, or
    \item $\mathsf w$ lies on $\beta_e$ and $e$ is white (in particular, this holds whenever $\mathsf w$ is $\beta_e$-outer).
\end{itemize}
\item Independent of the choice of convex vertex $v$.
\item Invariant under changing the choice of $R^{\mathrm{in}}_\mathsf x$ for an outer vertex $\mathsf x$ whenever it is not unique.

\end{enumerate}

