[Prop branching-rules of arXiv:2510.23430, 'On the Martin boundary for discrete TASEP'] There exist symmetric functions $G^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda/\mu}\in\hat\Lambda$, $g^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda/\mu}\in\Lambda$ satisfying the following properties:
\begin{itemize}
\item One variable specializations $G^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda/\mu}(x), g^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda/\mu}(x)$ coincide with (equation Gone of the paper), (equation gone of the paper);

\item $G^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda/\varnothing}=G^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda}$, $g^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda/\varnothing}=g^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda}$;

\item The following branching rules hold for any sets of variables $\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}$
$$
G_{\lambda/\mu}^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y})=\sum_{\nu}G^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda/\nu}(\mathbf{x})G^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\nu/\mu}(\mathbf{y}),
$$
$$
g_{\lambda/\mu}^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y})=\sum_{\nu}g^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\lambda/\nu}(\mathbf{x})g^{(\mathfrak a,\mathfrak b)}_{\nu/\mu}(\mathbf{y}),
$$
\end{itemize}

