[Theorem thm:mf_meas of arXiv:2412.05239, 'Conditions for uniform in time convergence: applications to averaging, numerical discretis'] Suppose (ass_meanfield of the paper) holds. Let $\nu$ be a probability measure on $\mathbb{R}^d$. Then, there exists a constant $\tilde{\mathbf{C}}>0$ such that for all $t\ge 0$ and $N\in \mathbb{N}$
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{dist} ((\nu \bar{p}_t)^{\otimes N}, \nu^{\otimes N} p_t^N) \le \tilde{\mathbf{C}} N^{-\alpha}\, .
\end{align*}
Moreover, the constant $\tilde{\mathbf{C}}$ depends on $\lambda$, $C$ and $\mathbf{C}(\nu)$ given in (ass_meanfield of the paper) and is independent of $N\in\mathbb{N}$.

[Proposition compapinningz of arXiv:2505.13382, 'The localization transition for the directed polymer in a random environment is smooth'] Given $p\in[1,2]$, there exit constants $C,u_0>0$ such that for all $u\in[0,u_0]$,
\begin{equation}
 {\ensuremath{\mathbb E}} \left[ \left(W^{\beta_c+u}_n\right)^{p}\right] \le \sum_{k\ge 0} \sum_{i_1<\dots<i_k\le n} (C u^{\frac{p}{2}})^{k}{\ensuremath{\mathbb E}} \left[ \left(W^{\beta_c}_{n-i_k}\right)^{p}\right] \prod_{j=1}^k{\ensuremath{\mathcal K}} ^{(p)}(i_{j}-i_{j-1})
\end{equation}
with the convention that $i_0=0$ (both $C$ and $u_0$ may depend on $\beta$ and of the distribution of $\omega$). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

