[Proposition prop:ik-partial-duality-2 of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] $\textbf{IK} \vdash \neg \Diamond \varphi \to \Box \neg \varphi$.
  \begin{proof}
    Follows from the following derivation.
    \begin{center}
      \AxiomC{$ $}
      \UnaryInfC{$(\Diamond \varphi \to \bot) \to (\bot \to \Box \bot) \to (\Diamond \varphi \to \Box \bot)$}
      \UnaryInfC{$(\Diamond \varphi \to \bot) \to (\Diamond \varphi \to \Box \bot)$}

      \AxiomC{$ $}
      \RightLabel{\scriptsize{$\textsf{FS2}$}}
      \UnaryInfC{$(\Diamond \varphi \to \Box \bot) \to \Box (\varphi \to \bot)$}

      \BinaryInfC{$(\Diamond \varphi \to \bot) \to \Box (\varphi \to \bot)$}
      \UnaryInfC{$\neg \Diamond \varphi \to \Box \neg \varphi$}
      \DisplayProof
    \end{center}
    We note that no modal principles are used except for $\textsf{FS2}$.
  \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

