[Theorem thm:gamarnik_giant of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Under the assumptions of Theorem (thm:giants_MCMs of the paper), assume further:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If a pair $(i,j)$ is BAD, then $\mathbf d_{(i,j)}(n)$ and $\mathbf d_{(j,i)}(n)$ are null sequences for all $n \geq 1$, i.e., they only have zero entries.
        \item  Considering the natural offspring matrix $M$ on $S^2 \times S^2$ where the $((i_1,j_1),(i_2,j_2))$-th entry is the number $\Exp{D_{(i_1,j_1,j_2)}}\1_{\{j_1=i_2\}}$, there exists a $p\geq 1$ such that all entries of $M^p$ are strictly positive.
    \end{itemize}
    Then if $\eta<1$, $\frac{|\mathcal C^{(1)}_n|}{n}\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mathbb{P}}{\rightarrow}} 1-\eta$, while if $\eta=1$, then $\frac{|\mathcal C^{(1)}_n|}{n}\ensuremath{\stackrel{\mathbb{P}}{\rightarrow}} 0$. Additionally, if $\eta<1$, the second largest component $\mathcal C^{(2)}_n$ satisfies $|\mathcal C^{(2)}_n|=O(\log n)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 32 of arXiv:2604.22159, 'Adapted optimal transport between filtered Gaussian processes'] [Adapted Brenier coupling between elements of $\cFG$] 
Let $\bX = \bG^{a, L}$ and $\bY = \bG^{b, M}$ be elements of $\cFG$. An adapted Brenier coupling between $\bX$ and $\bY$ is an element $\pi \in \Cpl_{\mathrm{bc}}(\bX, \bY)$ such that for all $t \in [N]$ and ($\pi$-almost) all $\omega_{1:(t-1)}^{\bX}, \omega_{1:(t-1)}^{\bY} \in \bR^{(t-1)d}$, the conditional distribution
\begin{equation} 
\pi_{t}(\dd \omega_{t}^{\bX}, \dd \omega_{t}^{\bY} \mid \omega^{\bX}_{1:(t-1)}, \omega^{\bY}_{1:(t-1)})
\end{equation}
of $(\epsilon_{t}^{X}, \epsilon_{t}^{Y})$ given $(\epsilon_{1:(t-1)}^X, \epsilon_{1:(t-1)}^Y) = (\omega^{\bX}_{1:(t-1)}, \omega^{\bY}_{1:(t-1)})$ solves the optimal transport problem
\begin{equation}
\inf_{\gamma \in \Cpl(\cN_d(0, I), \cN_d(0, I))} \int \| (\hat{X}_{t} + L_{t,t}\omega_{t}^{\bX}) - (\hat{Y}_{t} + M_{t,t}\omega_{t}^{\bY}) \|_2^2  \gamma(\dd \omega_{t}^{\bX}, \dd \omega_{t}^{\bY}).
\end{equation}

[Proposition of arXiv:2509.23069, 'Every cutoff profile is possible'] Consider the sequence of FIT Markov chains $((Y^{(n)}_t)_{t\geq 0})_{n\geq 8}$ defined above. For every integer $q\geq 2$ and every $c\in {\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} $, as $n\to \infty$ we have 
    \begin{equation}
        2^{q}\left(  \mathrm{d}^{(n)}\left( \left\lfloor 3n + c n^{1/q} \right\rfloor\right) - \frac{1}{2}\right) \to \begin{cases}
            2 & \text{ if } c\leq -1, \\
            (1-x) & \text{ if } -1<c<0, \\ 
            0 & \text{ if } c = 0, \\
            (-1-x) & \text{ if } 0<c<1, \\
            -2 & \text{ if } c\geq 1. \\
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

