[Proposition prop:moderate:cold of arXiv:2607.04047, 'Top Singular Value in Sum-Products of Random Matrices'] Assume that $\beta= \Omega(1)$, $\log m = o(N^{1/3})$ and $N = o(n^3)$. Then there exist some universal constants $C, c > 0$ such that for every $\epsilon \in (0,1)$,
\begin{align*}
    {\mathbb P}\Big( 
         \mfrac{|\mathcal{E} - \bar \mathcal{E}|}{\beta^2 \log m}  
        \;>\;
        C \Big( \mfrac{\epsilon}{(1-\epsilon) \beta \log m}
            +
            \mfrac{1}{\beta (\log m)^{1/4}} \Big)
    \Big)
    \;\leq\; 
    (1+ \epsilon^{-2}) e^{ - c  \, (\log m)^{3/4} }\;.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 32 of arXiv:2605.31088, 'On couplings for kinetic Langevin diffusions'] For $h>0$, $n\in\N$, let $A_n$ and $\Sigma_{n}$ given by \eqref{eq: OBABO n step mean} and \eqref{eq: OBABO n step covariance}. Then we have the upper bound
    \begin{equation*}
        \l\|\Sigma_{n}^{-1/2}A^n\r\|\leq\br{\frac{44}{\gamma (hn)^3}+\frac{264+44\gamma^2}{\gamma hn}}^{1/2}.
    \end{equation*}

