body:
[Proposition eq:lower-gamma2 of arXiv:2608.11378, 'On the most likely geodesic in last passage percolation'] Suppose the weight distribution satisfies Assumptions~(ass:lower-tail of the paper) and (ass:tiltable of the paper). For every $\delta > 0$ and all sufficiently large $n$,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}(L(\gamma^{\raisebox{-0.5ex}{$\scriptstyle\ulcorner$}})= L_n)\le \exp\bigl(-dn(I(\mu)-\delta)\bigr).
\end{equation}

[Definition 6 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Fix $q \in (0,1)$, $c \in (1, q^{-1})$, and define
\begin{equation}
\bar{\kappa} = \frac{(c-q)^2}{(1-qc)^2} - 1 > 0, \hspace{2mm} p^{\mathrm{top}} = \frac{q}{c-q}, \hspace{2mm} C^{\mathrm{top}} = \frac{q (c^2 - 2qc + 1)}{(c-q)(1-qc)}.
\end{equation}
Let $N \in \mathbb{N}$, and $\lpplinen_{1}$ be as in (\ref{Eq.Deflpplen}). Define the rescaled process $\mathcal{U}_1^{\mathrm{top},N}$ on $[0, \bar{\kappa})$ by
$$\mathcal{U}_1^{\mathrm{top},N}(t) = [p^{\mathrm{top}} (1 + p^{\mathrm{top}})]^{-1/2} N^{-1/2} \left( \lpplinen_{1}(tN) - C^{\mathrm{top}} N -  p^{\mathrm{top}} t N   \right) \mbox{ for } t \in [0, \bar{\kappa}).$$

[Definition 127 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] For any $x \in [0, a]$ and $u \in \mathbb{C}$, let $z_x = z_x (u) = z(x, u) \in \mathbb{C}$ solve the equation (with $f_x(z)$ as in \eqref{fxz})
	\begin{flalign}

		\partial_x z_x = \displaystyle\frac{f_x(z_x)}{f_x (z_x) - 1}, \quad \text{where $z_0 = u$}.
	\end{flalign}

[Theorem thm:mixing of arXiv:2607.22991, 'Mixing times and spectra of non-equilibrium symmetric exclusion processes on general graph'] For any SEP with arbitrary heat baths on an $n$-vertex connected graph, the mixing time is $O(\tau_{\rm hit}\log n)$
and is also $\Omega(\tau_{\rm hit}/\log n)$.  More precisely:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\tau_{\rm mix}(\varepsilon)\le 2\log_2(n\varepsilon^{-1})\tau_{\rm hit}$ for any $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$;
\item[(b)] $\tau_{\rm mix}(1/40) > \tau_{\rm hit}/a\ln n$ for some universal constant~$a>0$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

