[Lemma lem: Martingale of arXiv:2606.28031, 'Insurance risk models in a heterogeneous time-dependent population: scaling limits and rui'] Assume there exists $\overline{\alpha}>0$ such that both $\mathbb{E}[{\mathrm e}^{-\overline{\alpha} X(1)}]$ and $\mathbb{E}[{\mathrm e}^{-\overline{\alpha} Y(1)}]$ are finite. Then, for any $\alpha \in [0,\overline{\alpha}]$, the process $M_\alpha$ defined by
\[
M_\alpha(t) := \exp{{\left(- \alpha V(t) - k_\alpha(t)\right)}}
\] 
is a càdlàg $\PAR{\mathcal{F}_t}_{t\geqslant 0}$-martingale, with $M_\alpha(0)=1$. It is also an $\PAR{\mathcal{F}_t \vee \mathcal{F}^F}_{t\geqslant 0}$-martingale.

Moreover, if $\tau$ is a bounded $\PAR{\mathcal{F}_t \vee \mathcal{F}^F}_{t\geqslant 0}$-stopping time, then
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\big[M_\alpha(\tau)\,\vert\, \mathcal{F}^F\big] = 1, \quad \text{almost surely.}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

