[Definition 10 of arXiv:2606.30364, 'Distributional comparison for non-commutative infinitely divisible probability measures'] Let $(\Ac,\varphi)$ be a $C^{\ast}$-probability space. 

The \emph{non-commutative $p$-norm} $\abs{\, \cdot \,}_p :  \Ac \rightarrow  \R_{\geq 0}$ is defined for each $a \in  \Ac$  by 


	\begin{align}
		\abs{\, a \,}_p : =

	 	\sqrt[p]{  \, \varphi[ (a^* a)^{p/2} ] \ \ } .
	\end{align}


In particular, for $p=1$, we have \begin{align} 	\abs{\, a \, }_1 = 	\varphi[ (a^* a)^{1/2} ] . \end{align}

[Theorem a.s & L^2 convergence of arXiv:2505.05921, 'Limit Theorems for step reinforced random walks\\ with regularly varying memory'] {}
     Let $\{S_n\}_{n\geq 0}$ be the SRRW-RVM with the zero mean innovation sequence $\{\xi_n\}_{n\ge 1}$ satisfying $\mathbb{E}\xi_1^2=1$. If  $\{v_n\}$ is bounded and $p\in[p_c,1]$, then
     \begin{equation*}
         a_n\mu_nS_n\to \frac{p(\gamma+1)}{p(\gamma+1)-\gamma}M_\infty, \quad \text{ in $L^2$ and a.s.},
     \end{equation*}
     where $M_\infty$ is the almost sure and $L^2$ limit of the martingale $M_n$, defined in Proposition~(Asymptotics of martingale M_n of the paper).

     Additionally, when the innovation sequence is symmetric Rademacher, the limiting random variable $M_\infty$ is platykurtic, that is, it has kurtosis strictly smaller than $3$ and is not Gaussian. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 14 (Exit and entrance times) of arXiv:2506.09462, 'Transition Path Theory For L\'{e}vy-Type Processes: SDE Representation and Statistics'] Given a trajectory $\{X_t\}_{t \geq 0}$ starting from $x_0 \in A$, the last-exit-$A$-before-entrance-$B$ time $\tau_{A,n}^-$ and the first-entrance-$B$-after-exit-$A$ time $\tau_{B,n}^+$ belong to $\mathbb{T}$ if and only if
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{aligned}
            \lim_{t \uparrow \tau_{A,n}^-} X_t = x_n^A \in \bar{A}, \quad X_{\tau_{B,n}^+} \in \bar{B}, \quad \mbox{and}\quad X_s \notin A \cup B\quad
            \forall s \in (\tau_{A,n}^-, \tau_{B,n}^+).
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}
    Similarly, the last-exit-$B$-before-entrance-$A$ time $\tau_{B,n}^-$ and the first-entrance-$A$-after-exit-$B$ time $\tau_{A,n}^+$ belong to $\mathbb{T}$ if and only if
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{aligned}
            \lim_{t \uparrow \tau_{B,n}^-} X_t = x_n^B \in \bar{B}, \quad X_{\tau_{A,n}^+} \in \bar{A}, \quad\mbox{and}\quad X_s \notin A \cup B \quad
            \forall s \in (\tau_{B,n}^-, \tau_{A,n}^+).
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}

[Lemma lemma4 of arXiv:2411.01731, '$L^2$-Wasserstein contraction of modified Euler schemes for SDEs with high diffusivity and'] Assume that   $({\bf A}_0)$, $({\bf A}_5)$ and (equation EE11- of the paper) hold.
Then, there is   a constant   $  c^\star >0$
 such that for any $t\ge s\ge0$ with $|t-s|\le1$, and $\delta\in(0,\delta^\star_3],$
\begin{equation}
	\mathbb E	|X_t-X_{t}^{\delta}|^2\le  c^\star\Big( \big(
 d^{\ell_{\star\star}}
	+\mathbb E|X_0|^{4(1+\ell_0)}+\mathbb E|X_0^\delta|^{p_\star}\big)\delta^2+\mathbb E|X_s-X_s^{\delta}|^2\Big)
\end{equation}
 provided $X_0\in L^{4(1+\ell_0)}(\Omega\rightarrow\mathds R^d,\mathscr F_0,\mathbb P)$
and $X_0^\delta\in L^{p_\star}(\Omega\rightarrow\mathds R^d,\mathscr F_0,\mathbb P)$, where positive constants $\delta_3^\star, p_\star, \ell_{\star\star}$ were defined in (equation DD6 of the paper).

