[Thm 3 (\cite{Che26}*{Theorem 1.13}) of arXiv:2608.26723, 'Extrinsic upper bounds for the harmonic mean of eigenvalues in curved manifolds'] Let $M$ be an $m$-dimensional closed submanifold in $\mathbb{R}^n(c)$. Then the first $m$ nonzero eigenvalues of the Laplacian satisfy
\begin{equation}
	\mathfrak{H}(\lambda_1,\cdots, \lambda_m) \le \frac{m}{\vol(M)}\int_M\left(c+|\mathbf{H}|^2\right),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation*}
	\mathfrak{H}(\lambda_1,\cdots,\lambda_m)=\Big(\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^m\lambda_i^{-1}\Big)^{-1}
\end{equation*}
represents the harmonic mean of the first $m$ nonzero eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\cdots,\lambda_m$ of the Laplacian on $M$.

Equality holds if and only if $M$ is either minimally immersed in $\mathbb{S}^n$ by a subspace of the first eigenspace, or minimally immersed in a geodesic sphere of $\mathbb{R}^{n}(c)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop equation:consistency of arXiv:2411.12717, 'Excursion theory for Markov processes indexed by Lévy trees'] Fix a  continuous function $h:\mathbb{R}_+\to \mathbb{R}_+$ with $\sigma_h \in(0, \infty)$  and  $h(0) = h(\sigma_h) = 0$. There exists  a unique  measure  $\mathbf{Q}^h_{x,r}({\mathrm{d}} \overline{W})$ on $\mathcal{W}_{\overline{E}}^{\mathbb{R}_+}$ such that, for every  $n \geq 1$, $A_0, A_1,  \dots, A_n$ measurable subsets of $\mathcal{W}_{\overline{E}}$ and  $0 = t_0 < t_1 < t_2 \dots < t_n$, we have 
\begin{align}
      \mathbf{Q}^h_{x,r} \Big( &  \overline{W}_{t_0}\in A_0, \, \overline{W}_{t_1} \in A_1,  \dots, \overline{W}_{t_n}\in A_n\Big) \nonumber
   \\&= \mathbbm{1}_{A_0}(x,r)  \int_{A_1} \nu_{t_1}^h({\mathrm{d}} \overline{\mathrm{w}}_1) \int_{A_2\times \dots\times A_n} \bar{R}_{ m_{h}(t_1,t_2),h(t_2) }(\overline{\mathrm{w}}_1,{\mathrm{d}} \overline{\mathrm{w}}_2) \dots \bar{R}_{ m_{h}(t_{n-1},t_n),h(t_n) }(\overline{\mathrm{w}}_{n-1},{\mathrm{d}} \overline{\mathrm{w}}_n) .
\end{align}

