[Theorem thm:smoothed-learning of arXiv:2512.04371, 'Constructive Approximation under Carleman's Condition, \\ with Applications to Smoothed An'] Fix $\sigma,\varepsilon>0$ and $\delta\in (0,1)$. 
  There exists an algorithm that learns $\cH(k)$ in the $\sigma$-smoothed agnostic setting with excess error $\varepsilon$ and confidence $1-\delta$ using  i.i.d. samples of
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item size $n = O\Big(\varepsilon^{-2}  \big(  d^{ O((\frac{k\log (k/\sigma)+  \log(1/\varepsilon)}{\sigma^2})^{r/2})} + \log(1/\delta)\big)\Big)$ under $r$-strictly sub-exponential input;
    \item size $n = O\Big(\varepsilon^{-2}  \big( d^{(\varepsilon^{-1} k^k)^{O(\sigma^{-1})}}+ \log(1/\delta)\big)\Big)$ under sub-exponential input.
  \end{enumerate}

