[Proposition 36 of arXiv:2606.15425, 'Well-posedness of stochastic parabolic equations with gradient nonlinearities and applicat'] Let $z_0 \in L^0(\Omega;B^{d/q}_{q,2p})$ be $\mathcal F_0$-measurable and take values in a compact subset $\mathcal A \Subset B^{d/q}_{q,2p}$. Given a measurable process $c \colon \Omega \times [0,\tau] \rightarrow \mathcal X_c$, there exists a 
deterministic $t_0 > 0$ and unique mild solution $z$ of 
\begin{equation} 
\d z_t  = \left(\Delta z_t - |\nabla z_t|^2 + \widetilde h(z_t,c_t) + \widetilde\Phi(z_t,c_t) |\nabla z_t|\right) \d t
\end{equation}
with $$z \in L^\infty(\Omega;\mathbb Z_q(\tau_0)),$$ where $\tau_0 = \tau \wedge t_0$. In particular, for $(z,c)$ given by Lemma \ref{lemma:phasefieldlocalstrongex}, $z \in \mathbb Z_q(\tau_0)$. Moreover, if $z_0\geq 0$, $\mathbb P$-a.s., then $z_t \geq 0$ for all $t \in [0,t_0 \wedge \tau)$, $\mathbb P$-a.s.

[Lemma Eq:5.2 of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] Let~$\alpha\in\mathbb R$ and, given $\{w_1(q)\}_{q\in\mathbb Z}\in\ell^1(\mathbb Z)$, define
$\{w_k(\cdot)\}_{k=1}^{ n+1}$ by the recursion
\begin{equation}

w_{k+1}(q):=\frac{a_{k-1}(0)^b}{a_{k}(0)}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}
\ell_1,\dots,\ell_b\in\mathbb Z\\\ell_1+\dots+\ell_b=q
\end{subarray}}
\biggl(\,\prod_{i=1}^{b-1}\frac{a_{k-1}(\ell_i)}{a_{k-1}(0)}\biggr)\,w_k(\ell_b)\,\theta_{k}^{(q+\alpha)^2}.
\end{equation}
Then $\{w_k(q)\}_{q\in\mathbb Z}\in\ell^1(\mathbb Z)$ for all~$k=1,\dots, n+1$  and so we can set 
\begin{equation}

f_k(z) := a_{k-1}(0)\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{v_{k-1}(z)}\sum_{q\in\mathbb Z}w_k(q)\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{2\pi\text{\rm  i}\mkern0.7mu qz}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, with $\mathcal F_k$ as in (equation Eq:4.1 of the paper),
\begin{equation}

E\bigl(\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{2\pi\text{\rm  i}\mkern0.7mu\alpha \phi_k}f_k(\phi_k)\,\big|\,\mathcal F_{k+1}\bigr) =
\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{2\pi\text{\rm  i}\mkern0.7mu\alpha \phi_{k+1}}f_{k+1}(\phi_{k+1})
\end{equation}
then holds for all~$k=1,\dots,n$. In short, $\{\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{2\pi\text{\rm  i}\mkern0.7mu\alpha \phi_k}f_k(\phi_k)\}_{k=1}^{ n+1}$ is a reverse martingale. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

