for $N$ large enough. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem 7 of arXiv:2609.01101, '2609.01101'] For every $\epsilon>0$,
\begin{equation*}
|c_{\mathbf b}(n)|\ll_{\epsilon}n^{\epsilon},
\end{equation*}
uniformly for real $\mathbf b$.  If $p\notin S_L$, then
\begin{equation}
c_{\mathbf b}(p)=
\sum_{i=1}^r q_i\chi_i(\Frob_p)p^{-ib_i}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for $\sigma>1$,
\begin{equation}
\log\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\frac{|c_{\mathbf b}(n)|^2}{n^\sigma}
=\sum_p\frac{|c_{\mathbf b}(p)|^2}{p^\sigma}+O(1),
\end{equation}
where the error is uniform for $\sigma>1$ and for real
$\mathbf b$.

[Theorem 1 (Existence in the strictly positive regime) of arXiv:2605.23529, 'Generalized Bessel-Dunkl diffusions'] Let $x_0\in\Cc$. Assume \ref{ass:cont}, \ref{ass:sigma}, and \ref{ass:k:positive}. Then there exists a weak solution $X=(X_1,\ldots,X_N)$ of \eqref{eq:main:SDE}, started from $x_0$, which takes values in $\Cc$ up to its lifetime $\life{X}>0$. More precisely, there exist a filtered probability space carrying an $N$-dimensional Brownian motion $B$ and a continuous $\Cc$-valued semimartingale $X=(X_1,\ldots,X_N)$ such that
    \begin{align}
        X(t)
            & = x_0 + \int_0^t \sigma(s,X(s))\,dB(s) + \int_0^t b(s,X(s))\,ds  +\sum_{\alpha\in R_+}\alpha \int_0^t \frac{k_\alpha(s,X(s))}{\langle X(s),\alpha\rangle}\,ds

    \end{align}
    for every $t\in[0,\life{X})$. Moreover, the singular integrals in \eqref{eq:thm:existence-sde} are finite on compact subintervals of $[0,\life{X})$, with the usual improper interpretation at the initial time if $x_0\in\Cb$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 19 (Divisor obstruction) of arXiv:2607.16724, 'Algebraic Transfer for Operator-Valued Gaussian Chaoses: Oriented Schatten Profiles and Si'] Let $1<r<\infty$.
For $N\ge2$, let
\begin{equation}

 D_N:=\operatorname{diag}(1,2^{-1/r},\ldots,N^{-1/r}).
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}

 \norm{D_N}_{r,\infty}^{*}=1,
 \qquad
 \norm{D_N}_{\Sch_{r,\infty}}\le\frac r{r-1},
\end{equation}
whereas, for every $0\le\alpha<1/r$,
\begin{equation}

 \norm{D_N\otimes D_N}
  _{\Sch_{r,\infty}(\log\Sch)^{-\alpha}}
 \ge c_{r,\alpha}
       (\log(e+N))^{1/r-\alpha}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, neither
$\Sch_{r,\infty}\times\Sch_{r,\infty}\to\Sch_{r,\infty}$ nor a
logarithmic enlargement with exponent $\alpha<1/r$ can hold uniformly over
matrix dimensions for external tensor products.

[Lemma 2 of arXiv:2608.19560, '\textbf{Computing transient probabilities in Markovian queues with balking conditional on '] $\bm{q}(u,K)$ is determined by the following linear equation 

