[Lemma T_prop_lemma_eq of arXiv:2606.17425, 'Order statistics for edge eigenvectors of Wigner matrices'] For any $k \geq 1$, $\mathring{V}$ satisfies the following properties \begin{equation}
			\sum_{b = 1}^N (\mathring{V}^k)_{ab} = 0, \quad \norm{\mathring{V}^k}_{\max} \leq \frac{C_0}{N},

		\end{equation} where $C_0 = 2(C_{\mathrm{sup}} + 1)$ with $C_{\mathrm{sup}} \geq C_{\mathrm{inf}} > 0$ given in (equation mean_field_condition of the paper). Furthermore, there exists a constant $c_0$ with $C_{\mathrm{inf}} \leq c_0 \leq 1$ such that \begin{equation}
			\norm{\mathring{V}^k}_{\max} \leq \norm{\mathring{V}^k} \leq (1 - c_0)^k. 

		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary theorem: affine_weigh of arXiv:2411.04238, 'Holomorphic jump-diffusions'] Let $X=(X_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$ be an $S$-valued jump-diffusion with characteristics $(b,a,K)$ and extended generator $\mathcal A$. Fix a weight function $v$ in the sense of Definition~(def2 of the paper). Assume that $b_j,a_{ij}\in\mathcal O(S)$, $K$ is a kernel with holomorphic jump size, and let $F$ be the corresponding non-negative measure on the measurable space $E$.  Fix $\varepsilon>0$ and suppose that for each $z\in P^d_{R(S)+\varepsilon}(0)$, there exists $\delta_z>0$ such that
 \begin{align}
         &\int_E  \ \sup_{w\in P^d_{\delta_z}(z)}  \|j_\varepsilon(w, y)\|\land\|j_\varepsilon(w, y)\|^2 \ F(d y)<\infty,\\
  &\int_E  \sup_{w\in P^d_{\delta_z}(z)} 1_{\{\|j_\varepsilon(w, y)\|> 1\}} \exp(m \ v(\|w+j_\varepsilon(w, y)\|))\ F(d y)<\infty,
  \end{align}
  for some $m\in \mathbb R_+$. Let $G\in (0,\infty]^d$ satisfy (equation eqnG of the paper) and set
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{V}:=H_v(P^d_G(0)) \qquad \text{and}\qquad 
     \mathcal{V}^*:=\{\mathbf{u}\in \mathcal{S}^* \colon h_\mathbf{u}=h|_{{P}^d_{R(S)}(0)},  \ h\in \mathcal{V}\}.\nonumber 
\end{align*}
Then $X$ is an $S$-valued $\mathcal{V}$-affine-holomorphic process and for all $\mathbf{u}\in\mathcal{V}^*$, $$\mathcal{A}\exp(h_\mathbf{u})=\exp(h_{\mathbf{u}})R(h_\mathbf{u})|_S,$$ where $R:\mathcal{V}^*\rightarrow \mathcal{S}^*$ is given by
(equation eq:R1nice of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Dfn 62 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] The \textbf{almost periodic} (`\textbf{AP}') \textbf{entropy of $\phi$ along $\bs{\pi}$} is the quantity
\begin{equation}
 \rmh_{\bspi}(\phi) := \inf_O \limsup_{n \to\infty} \frac{1}{d_n}\log \frac{\vol_{2kd_n}\X(\pi_n,O)}{v(d_n)^k},
\end{equation}
where the infimum runs over all neighbourhoods of $\phi$ in $\B(\A,\rmM_k)_+$.

[Corollary 25 of arXiv:2608.20613, 'Random walks on wreath products \\and spectral gaps for coloured interchange processes'] Let $A$ be a finite abelian group of order $r$.  Every minimal gap-determining family for $A\wr\Sn_n$ has cardinality
\[
\frac12\left(
\binom{n+r-1}{r-1}
+
[t^n](1-t)^{-\abs{\widehat A[2]}}
(1-t^2)^{-(r-\abs{\widehat A[2]})/2}
\right).
\]

[Lemma lem:concavitytwoterms of arXiv:2601.13426, 'A uniformity principle for spatial matching'] Under the same conditions as~(prop:strongconcave1D-shift of the paper),
    \begin{align}
        \underbrace{
            \widetilde{\delta}_m(r_1 + \tau,\mathsf{R}) - \widetilde{\delta}_m(r_1,\mathsf{R})}_{\triangleq \, \mathrm{(I)} 
        }
        \,-\, 
        \underbrace{
            \big( \widetilde{\delta}_m(r_2, \mathsf{R}) - \widetilde{\delta}_m(r_2 - \tau, \mathsf{R}) \big) }_{\triangleq \, \mathrm{(II)}     
        } 
        \geq {8} \,\alpha_{\xi, \gamma, \eta} \tau^3 - \frac{2\eta \, r_2}{n} \, . 
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Cor tree-worst-lower of arXiv:2609.03856, 'Consensus time for asynchronous $\ell^p$ relaxation: graph dependence'] For every $1<p<\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathsf T_p(G,1/2)
 \ge c_p n\max\left\{\log n,\frac{T_G}{\Delta}\right\}.
\end{equation}
The two terms in the maximum need not be realized by the same profile.

[Proposition prop:open-loop of arXiv:2608.09368, 'A proof of Ross's conjecture for two-site moving-target search'] For every $n\ge0$,
\begin{equation}
 V_n(p)=\min_{w\in\{1,2\}^n}J_w(p).

\end{equation}
Consequently $V_n$ is a continuous, piecewise-affine concave function
of $p$.

[Definition 8 of arXiv:2607.08314, 'Berry-Heisenberg random waves'] For $\alpha>0$ and $c\in \mathcal{O}^1$ fixed, we refer to the left-invariant Gaussian random field $\{  \xi_{\alpha,c} (g), \, g\in \Hei \}$ with zero mean and covariance 
     \begin{align*}
           \E\left[ \xi_{\alpha ,c} (g) \overline{\xi_{\alpha, c}(h)} \right] =2F_{\lambda}^{(c)} (h^{-1} g),
     \end{align*}
     as the  $(\alpha, c)$-Berry-Heisenberg random wave.

[Theorem TA1 of arXiv:2604.06012, 'Large fringe trees for random trees with given vertex degrees'] With notations as above, if (equation Lindeberg of the paper) holds, then, as \ensuremath{{\kappa\to\infty}},
\begin{align}
  \frac{S_{m_\kappa}-\operatorname{\mathbb E{}}S_{m_\kappa}}{\widehat{\sigma}_\kappa}
=   \frac{S_{m_\kappa}-m_\kappa\bar d_\kappa}{\widehat{\sigma}_\kappa}
\overset{\mathrm{d}}{\longrightarrow} \mathrm{N}(0,1).
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 11 (Enveloping at an integer mean: hypergeometric) of arXiv:2608.06232, 'The mean absolute deviation of the classical discrete distributions:\\ collapse identities'] Let $\mu=nK/N\in\Z$, $1\le\mu$, with $(\kappa,\eta)\in(0,1)^{2}$ (so $h=0$, $\nu=\mu=Na$).
	Then, exactly,
	\begin{equation}
		\log\frac{\E|X-\mu|}{\sqrt{2Nad/\pi}}
		=\int_0^\infty\varphi(t)\,\Delta_N(t)\,dt,
	\end{equation}
	with
	\[
	\begin{aligned}
		\Delta_N(t)=
		& e^{-Kt}+e^{-(N-K)t}+e^{-nt}+e^{-(N-n)t}\\
		&-\Bigl[e^{-Nat}+e^{-Nbt}+e^{-Nct}+e^{-Ndt}+e^{-Nt}\Bigr],
	\end{aligned}
	\]
	and $\Delta_N(t)<0$ for every $t>0$.  The expansion \eqref{eq:hyp-mad} collapses at $h=0$
	to the odd series with coefficients
	\[
	\begin{aligned}
		\tilde a_{2j-1}(0)
		&=\frac{B_{2j}}{(2j-1)2j}
			\Bigl[W_{2j-1}-1-\bigl(a^{1-2j}+b^{1-2j}+c^{1-2j}+d^{1-2j}\bigr)\Bigr],\\
		(-1)^{j}\,\tilde a_{2j-1}(0)&>0,
	\end{aligned}
	\]
	and its successive partial sums strictly bracket the left-hand side of
	\eqref{eq:hyp-binet}.

[Proposition prop:gram of arXiv:2609.01672, '\textbf{All-Minors Matrix-Tree Theory for Superport Networks:\\ Completed Quotient-Inciden'] The superport response satisfies
\begin{equation}
L=(D^{T}K^{-1}D)^{-1}.
\end{equation}

[Prop p:xrigidity of arXiv:2411.10586, 'A convergence framework for Airy$_\beta$ line ensemble\\ via pole evolution'] For any ${\mathfrak d}, C_*>0$, there exist small $\delta,c>0$ and large $C>0$, such that the following holds.
Take any particle-generated $\{Y_t\}_{t\in{\mathbb R}}$ satisfying (a:SDE of the paper), and large $T>0$. Conditional on the event that $Y_{0}$ is $({\mathfrak d}, C_*)$-Airy-like,
with probability at least $1-e^{-c(\log T)^2}$, we have
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The poles of $\{Y_t\}_{t\in[T, 2T]}$ give a line ensemble $\{x_i(t)\}_{i\in \mathbb{N}, t\in[T,2T]}$, and  for each $t\in[T, 2T]$ and $w\in\mathbb{H}$,
    \begin{align}
        Y_t(w)=\sum_{i=1}^\infty\left(\frac{1}{x_i(t)-w}-\frac{1}{{\mathfrak a}_i}\right)-\frac{\Ai'(0)}{\Ai(0)}.
    \end{align}
    \item For each $t\in [T, 2T]$, $i\in\mathbb{N}$, $y\le C$, we have that  
\begin{equation}
|x_i(t)-{\mathfrak a}_i|\leq \frac{C(\log T)^{40}}{i^{\delta}},
\end{equation} 
and
\begin{equation}
|\{i\in\mathbb{N}:x_i(t)\in [y-1, y+1]\}|\leq C\sqrt{|y|+1}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2607.26944, 'Improved bounds on the inradius and inverse inradius process of the convex hull of planar '] Let \(W_i=(W_i(t):t\ge  0)\) be a standard one-dimensional Brownian motion, let $R_i$ be its range, and $\Theta_i$ be the inverse range. Then,
\[
\E\!\mleft[\min\{\Theta_1,\Theta_2\}^2\mright]
=
\frac13-\mathcal C, \quad \text{where}\quad \mathcal C
\coloneqq 
\frac{16}{\pi^6}
\sum_{\substack{a\ge  1\\ a\ {\rm odd}}}
\mleft[
\frac{20u_a}{a^6}
+
\frac{12\pi v_a u_a}{a^5}
+
\frac{\pi^2(2u_a-3u_a^2)}{a^4}
\mright],
\] with $u_a=\sech^2(\pi a/2)$, and $v_a=\tanh(\pi a/2)$. Moreover, every summand in the series defining \(\mathcal C\) is positive, yielding for any $N \in \N$, that 
\begin{equation*}
    \E\!\mleft[\min\{\Theta_1,\Theta_2\}^2\mright]
\le 
\frac13-\mathcal C_N , \quad \text{for}\quad \mathcal C_N
\coloneqq 
\frac{16}{\pi^6}
\sum_{\substack{1\le  a\le  N\\ a\ {\rm odd}}}
\mleft[
\frac{20u_a}{a^6}
+
\frac{12\pi v_a u_a}{a^5}
+
\frac{\pi^2(2u_a-3u_a^2)}{a^4}
\mright].
\end{equation*} Numerically, $\E\!\mleft[\min\{\Theta_1,\Theta_2\}^2\mright]
=
0.1493203187\ldots 
$.

[Definition 27 of arXiv:2606.16164, 'A Low-Regularity Semigroup Sewing Lemma via Quotient Structures'] Let  $\alpha\in\mathbb R$. A compatible frozen quotient family is a family $\mathfrak r=(\mathfrak r^\tau)_{\tau\in[0,T]}$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item for every $\tau\in[0,T]$, $\mathfrak r^\tau\in \mathfrak C^{1}_{\mathrm{log}}(\Delta^2_\tau;B_\alpha)$;

\item for every $0\le \tau'\le \tau\le T$, $\operatorname{Res}_{\tau,\tau'}\mathfrak r^\tau=\mathcal T_{\tau,\tau'}\mathfrak r^{\tau'}$;

\item the family is uniformly bounded in quotient seminorm $\sup_{\tau\in[0,T]}\|\mathfrak r^\tau\|_{\mathfrak C_{2, \mathrm{log}}^{1,\alpha}(\tau)}<\infty$.
\end{enumerate}
Denote by $\mathfrak Q_{\mathrm{fr}, \mathrm{log}}^{1,\alpha}$ the vector space of all compatible frozen quotient families, endowed with the seminorm $$\|\mathfrak r\|_{\mathfrak Q_{\mathrm{fr}, \mathrm{log}}^{1,\alpha}}:=\sup_{\tau\in[0,T]}\|\mathfrak r^\tau\|_{\mathfrak C_{2, \mathrm{log}}^{1,\alpha}(\tau)}.$$

[Prop dodgy-a of arXiv:2608.26876, 'Local Bousfield classes via homological support'] Suppose that every cohomological Bousfield class of $\cat T$ is a homological Bousfield class and let $A \in \cat T$. If $\LAT$ has a cohomological Bousfield class which is not homological then there exists an object $a\in\cat T$ such that
	\begin{equation}
		0 \neq a \in \mathinner{\left\langle A \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} \subseteq \mathinner{\left\langle a \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}.
	\end{equation}

[Theorem 1 (Exact weak closure at fixed order) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] For every $q,d<\infty$ and $R>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \overline{\mathfrak C_{q,d}(R)}^{\,w}
 =
 \bigcup_{E=E_1\oplus\cdots\oplus E_q}
 \left\{
  \Law(F):F\in\cW_q(E)^d,
  \ \E\abs{F}^2\le R^2
 \right\},}

\end{equation}
where the union ranges over separable graded real Hilbert spaces.
Moreover, every terminal on the right admits a realizing sequence of
homogeneous $q$-fold Wiener integrals for which every fixed loop-free
contraction diagram converges to its weighted block-pairing value.

For each represented tensorial terminal, the graded one-particle support
used by $F$ has a unique minimal closed realization.  It is separable and
is unique up to a grade-preserving orthogonal transformation.  This last
statement is relative to the represented tensorial terminal; the marginal
law alone need not determine the grading or the support. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem 6 (Stopping Time Criterion) of arXiv:2606.04245, 'Properties of a Special Type of Filtration and its Martingale Criteria'] Let $(T_n)_{n\geq1}$ be an increasing sequence of stopping times with respect to $\FF$ such that $T_n\to\infty$ almost surely as $n\to\infty$. 
    Assume that $P(\gamma<\infty)=1$ (i.e., $t_G<\infty)$ and $P(T_n<\gamma)>0$ for all $n\geq1$.
    Then there exists an increasing sequence of stopping times $(R_n)_{n\geq1}$ with respect to $\FF$ such that:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $R_n\leq t_G$ almost surely for all $n\geq1$;
        \item $R_n\to t_G$ almost surely as $n\to\infty$;
        \item For each $n$, the identity holds on the set before the jump:
        \begin{equation}
            \{T_n<\gamma\}=\{T_n=R_n<\gamma\}=\{R_n<\gamma\}.
        \end{equation}
    \end{itemize}

