body:
[Lemma 37 of arXiv:2608.17374, '\textbf{On the Pseudo-Mixing of Kac's Walk}'] Fix $\varepsilon\in(0,1/4)$.
There exists $c>0$ (depending only on $\beta$) such that for all sufficiently large $d$,
\begin{align}
\tau_{\mathrm{mix}}(d,\varepsilon)
&\;\ge\;
c\,\frac{d}{m}\bigl(\log d+\log(1/\varepsilon)\bigr),

\\
\tau_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(k)}(d,\varepsilon)
&\;\ge\;
c\,\frac{d}{m}\bigl(\log k+\log(1/\varepsilon)\bigr),
\qquad 1\le k\le d.

\end{align}

