[Definition 61 (Truncated two-dimensional Gaussian free field) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] For $N\geqs1$, let $\cK_N = \setsuch{k\in\Z^2}{\abs{k} \leqs N}$,
where $\abs{k} = \abs{k_1} + \abs{k_2}$. 
The \emph{truncated two-dimensional Gaussian free field (GFF)} with covariance 
$(\id-\Delta_N)^{-1}$ on $\Lambda$ is defined as 
\begin{equation}
 \GFFN(x) := \sum_{k\in\cK_N}
 \frac{X_k}{\sqrt{\lambda_k}} e_k(x)\;.
\end{equation} 
Here $\Delta_N$ is the restriction of $\Delta$ to the subspace $E_N$ of $\H$
spanned by Fourier basis functions $e_k$ with $\abs{k} \leqs N$.

[Definition def:mixing of arXiv:2411.10333, 'Subcritical annulus crossing in spatial random graphs'] We say that \(\mathscr{G}\) is \emph{mixing} for \(\lambda>0\) if, for all \(|x|\ge 10\), we have 
  		\begin{equation*}\tag{\(\mathscr{M}\)}
  		 	\lim_{r\to\infty}\big|\operatorname{Cov}_\lambda\big(\1_{\mathcal{G}(r)}, \1_{\mathcal{G}(xr,r)}\big)\big| = 0.
  		\end{equation*}
  		We denote this property by \(\mathscr{M}_\lambda\). We say that the graph is \emph{uniformly mixing} for \(\lambda>0\), denoted by \(\overline{\mathscr{M}}_\lambda\), if, for any \(|x|\ge 10\), we have
  		\begin{equation*}\tag{\(\overline{\mathscr{M}}\)}	            \lim_{r\to\infty}\sup_{\lambda'<\lambda}\big|\operatorname{Cov}_{\lambda'}\big(\1_{\mathcal{G}(r)}, \1_{\mathcal{G}(xr,r)}\big)\big| = 0.
  		\end{equation*}
  		In some situations we require a polynomial decay of the correlations. If this is the case, we say that \(\mathscr{G}\) is \emph{polynomially mixing} for \(\lambda>0\) with exponent \(\xi=\xi_\lambda<0\) if, for any \(|x|\ge 10\), we have
  		\begin{equation*}\tag{\({{\mathscr{P}}\!\!{\mathscr{M}}}\)}
 		\limsup_{r\to\infty}\frac{\log\big|\operatorname{Cov}_{\lambda}\big(\1_{\mathcal{G}(r)}, \1_{\mathcal{G}(xr,r)}\big)\big|}{\log r}\leq \xi
 		\end{equation*}
 		and denote this property by \({{\mathscr{P}}\!\!{\mathscr{M}}}_\lambda^{\xi}\).

