{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1d6e05f4a8651b0cd0a6d62bdf07cbb9bb16046cbceeca77ae5d1aaafeeba4f8","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/1d/1d6e05f4a8651b0cd0a6d62bdf07cbb9bb16046cbceeca77ae5d1aaafeeba4f8.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:38c1b7833ff34bd7d630ea5052ab723e03f2e228bec8d06d084de046565cfd25","bundle_entry_key":"2608-10415-theorem-191-proposition-191","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_053","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_053.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-10415-proposition-191-proposition-191","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: According to the usual rule of matrix multiplication, the formula for the powers of the adjacency matrix $d\in M_N(0,1)$ is as follows:
\begin{eqnarray*}
(d^k)_{i_0i_k}
&=&\sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_{k-1}}d_{i_0i_1}d_{i_1i_2}\ldots d_{i_{k-1}i_k}\\
&=&\sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_{k-1}}\delta_{i_0-i_1}\delta_{i_1-i_2}\ldots\delta_{i_{k-1}-i_k}\\
&=&\sum_{i_1,\ldots,i_{k-1}}\delta_{i_0-i_1-\ldots-i_{k-1}-i_k}\\
