[Def1 definition:snakePath of arXiv:2411.12717, 'Excursion theory for Markov processes indexed by Lévy trees'] Fix $(\mu , {\mathrm{w}}) \in \Theta$.  An element  $(\upvarrho , \omega) \in 
\mathbb{D}(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{M}_f(\mathbb{R}_+) \times \mathcal{W}_{E})$  is called a snake path started from $(\mu , {\mathrm{w}})$ if the following properties hold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\emph{(S1)}]   $(\upvarrho_0 , \omega_0) = (\mu,  {\mathrm{w}})$  and $\omega\in \mathcal{S}^\circ_{\mathrm{w}}$;
    \item[\emph{(S2)}] For every  $s \geq 0$, we have  $(\upvarrho_s, \omega_s) \in \Theta$; in  particular   $H(\upvarrho) = \zeta(\omega)$.
\end{enumerate}
The family of snake paths started from some fixed $(\mu , {\mathrm{w}}) \in \Theta$ is denoted by $\mathcal{S}_{\mu , {{\mathrm{w}}}}$, and when $(\mu , {\mathrm{w}})$ is of the form $(0,y)$ for some $y \in E$, we simply write $\mathcal{S}_{y}$. 
We set 
\begin{equation*}
   \mathcal{S} := \bigcup_{(\mu , {\mathrm{w}})\in \Theta}\mathcal{S}_{\mu , {\mathrm{w}}} 
\end{equation*}
for the collection of snake paths.

[Theorem 20 (first-order expansion of the bias: the sharp rate) of arXiv:2605.13648, 'Sticky CIR process with potential: invariant measure and exact sampling'] Let the assumptions of \cref{thm:ula-bias} hold, and fix $\delta\in(1,2)$.  Then, for every bounded measurable $\eta\colon[0,\infty)\to\mathbb R$,
  \begin{equation}
    \pi_h(\eta)-\pi(\eta)\;=\;K_\star\,\hat\eta(0)\,h\abs{\log h } \;+\;O\bigl(h\,\snorm{\eta}_\infty\bigr),
    \qquad \hat\eta(0)\coloneqq \eta(0)-\pi(\eta),
  \end{equation}
  with the observable-independent constant
  \begin{equation}
    K_\star\;=\;\tfrac12\,(\delta-1)\,\beta\,G'(0)^2\,\pi(\{0\}) .
  \end{equation}
  Equivalently, $\pi_h=\pi+K_\star\,h\abs{\log h } \,(\delta_0-\pi)+O(h)$ in total variation. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:helping_lemma of arXiv:2412.04005, '$\SLE_{\kappa}(\rho)$ processes in the light cone regime on\\ Liouville quantum gravity'] {dms2014mating}}$]

For each $z \in \mathbf{H}$, we have that 
\begin{align*}
d\widetilde{\mathfrak {h}}_{t}(z) = \mathrm{Re}\frac{2}{\widetilde{f}_{t}(z)}dB_{t}
\end{align*}
For each $y,z \in \mathbf{H}$ distinct, we have that 
