[Corollary box of arXiv:2412.04396, 'Superdiffusive Scaling Limits for the Symmetric\\ Exclusion Process with Slow Bonds'] Fix $i\in{\mathbb T}_k$. Let $h:\Omega_{kn}\to{\mathbb R}$ be such that $h(\eta)=h(\eta^{x,x+1})$  for every $in-1 \leq x\leq (1+i)n-2$ and every $\eta\in\Omega_{nk}$. Then, for any $\delta>0$,
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{split}
			\int h(w((i+1)n-1)-w(in-1)) f d\nu\;\leq\; &\delta n^2 \sum_{x=in-1}^{(i+1)n-1}\xi^{nk}_{x,x+1}{\mathfrak D}_{x,x+1}(\sqrt{f};\nu)\,+\,\frac{8n^\beta}{\delta\varepsilon_0 n^2}\int h^2 fd\nu\\
			\, - \,&\big[\rho\big(\frac{i}{k}\big)-\rho\big(\frac{i-1}{k}\big)\big]\int hw(in-1)w(in)fd\nu.
		\end{split}
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem lem3.5 of arXiv:2411.11408, 'Multidimensional specific relative entropy between continuous martingales'] We have {
\begin{equation}\textstyle 
h_l(\mathcal L(M^{\Gamma_1})|\mathcal L(M^{\Gamma_2}))=\frac{1}{2}\Big(tr\big((\Gamma_2 \Gamma_2^T)^{-1}(\Gamma_1 \Gamma_1^T)\big)-l - \log \frac{\det (\Gamma_1 \Gamma_1^T)}{\det (\Gamma_2 \Gamma_2^T)}\Big) .
\end {equation}}

[Proposition of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] The rotation of angle $t\in\mathbb R$ in the plane diagonalizes as:
$$R_t=\frac{1}{2}\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\i&-i\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}e^{-it}&0\\0&e^{it}\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}1&-i\\1&i\end{pmatrix}$$
Over the real numbers this is impossible, unless $t=0,\pi$.

