body:
[Theorem 9 (Opposite-jump monotonicity) of arXiv:2609.02590, 'Directional Optimal Sub-Gamma Scales for Infinitely Divisible Laws'] Let $X$ and $Z$ be independent variables in the standing class.

\begin{enumerate}
\item If the L\'evy measure of $Z$ has no positive jumps, then
\begin{equation}
 \cplus(X+Z)\leq\cplus(X).

\end{equation}
\item If the L\'evy measure of $Z$ has no negative jumps, then
\begin{equation}
 \cminus(X+Z)\leq\cminus(X).

\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Suppose in addition that $0<\tplus(X)<\infty$ and
$\cplus(X)=1/\tplus(X)$. Under the assumption in part 1,
\begin{equation}
 \cplus(X+Z)=\cplus(X).

\end{equation}
The reflected boundary statement holds on the left.

