{"artifact_digest":"sha256:7c3ab9f2c8e8a7b86e9b468606b904cec31e2d1b5891d084c1f0db6a78d59fd7","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/7c/7c3ab9f2c8e8a7b86e9b468606b904cec31e2d1b5891d084c1f0db6a78d59fd7.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:c3c7e9e01719d7f8d7abb32e34ee2b1410144f51830ca203b89bf8aaac517cd0","bundle_entry_key":"2602-08591-theorem-79-bounded","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_065","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_065.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2602-08591-thm-79-bounded","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The lemma (sCondition of the paper) shows that the second term of (MainBound of the paper) is uniformly bounded in $N$. In fact, condition $0<\alpha<\frac 1 2$ and $s<\frac 1 2$ implies condition $s<2\alpha+\frac 1 2$. 
\par It remains to show that the first term of (MainBound of the paper) is uniformly bounded in $N$. 
    We have 
    \begin{align*}
         \sum_{0\leqslant n \leqslant N }&  \frac{2^{np(s-2\alpha)}}{2^{2N(1-p\alpha)}}\sum_{\substack{ 2^{-n+N-2} \leqslant n < m \leqslant 2^{-n+N-1}\\  2^{-n+N-2}\leqslant k , l \leqslant 2^{-n+N-1}  }}\frac{\mathbb{E}\left\vert A_N(k2^{-N}\xrightarrow{m2^{-N}} l2^{-N})-A_N(k2^{-N}\xrightarrow{n2^{-N}} l2^{-N})\right\vert^p}{\vert l-k \vert ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:6b67dcfa364d78ab6d3abb1c1518414dd1620ce1c0a54d6fad1f3d3092cab293","revision_digest":"sha256:76f0bd1555206502b12fae0d8ef1a933357767921797b875f51df0f1404a0230","source_id":"arxiv:2602.08591","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

