[Theorem thm:GLPP-H4p of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] The dressed observable satisfies the mixed diamond
equation
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{n-1, \mathbf{m},
\mathbf{a}-2\cdot\mathbf{1}}^{-1}
- q\mathcal{M}_{n, \mathbf{m}+\mathbf{1},
\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{1}}^{-1}\bigr)
\mathcal{M}_{n-1, \mathbf{m}+\mathbf{1},
\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{1}} \\
&\qquad - \mathcal{M}_{n, \mathbf{m},
\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{1}}^{-1}
\bigl(\mathcal{M}_{n, \mathbf{m}+\mathbf{1},
\mathbf{a}}
- q\mathcal{M}_{n-1, \mathbf{m},
\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{1}}\bigr) = 0,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
at every $(n, \mathbf{m}, \mathbf{a})$ with $n \ge 2$ and
$a_i > x_{m_i+1} + n + 1$ for all~$i$.

[Theorem thm:virtual of arXiv:2609.01672, '\textbf{All-Minors Matrix-Tree Theory for Superport Networks:\\ Completed Quotient-Inciden'] Let \(P\) be an oriented forest of \(q\) virtual edges on vertices of \(G\) (the virtual edges need not be physical edges and may involve interior vertices). Let \(D_P\) be its reduced incidence matrix after grounding \(g\), and put
\begin{equation}
Z_P=D_P^TK^{-1}D_P,\qquad \mathcal L_P=Z_P^{-1}.
\end{equation}
For each physical spanning forest \(F\), contract its components in \(P\) to obtain a quotient virtual-port multigraph \(H_F^P\) with reduced incidence \(B_F^P=Q_FD_P\). Define \(\chi_F^P(S)=\det B_F^P[:,S]\). Then every inverse-Gram minor satisfies
\begin{equation}
\det Z_P[A,B]
=\frac{1}{\tau_c(G)}\sum_{F\in\mathcal{F}_{r+1}}
\chi_F^P(A)\chi_F^P(B)w(F).
\end{equation}
If
\begin{equation}
\Phi_P:=\sum_{\substack{F\in\mathcal{F}_{q+1}\\E(P)\text{ spans a tree of }H_F^P}}w(F),
\end{equation}
then for \(|I|=|J|=k\), \(r=q-k\), let \(E_I\) denote the selector matrix in the inherited virtual-edge order and define
\begin{equation}
\widehat\chi_F^P(I):=\det\begin{pmatrix}B_F^P\\E_I^T\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{align}
\det\mathcal L_P[I,J]
&=\frac{1}{\Phi_P}
  \sum_{F\in\mathcal{F}_{r+1}}
  \widehat\chi_F^P(I)\widehat\chi_F^P(J)w(F).

\end{align}
All coefficients remain \(0\) or \(\pm1\), and a completed determinant is nonzero exactly when its complementary virtual-edge set is a spanning tree of \(H_F^P\).

