[Proposition prop:separation-nnb of arXiv:2608.29708, 'Intuitionistic and Constructive Modal Logics for Classical Modal Logicians'] $\mathcal{V}^e_\Box \subsetneq \mathcal{V}^e \cap \mathscr{L}_\Box$. In particular:
  (1) $\neg \neg \Box \bot \to \Box \bot \in \mathcal{V}^e$, and
  (2) $\neg \neg \Box \bot \to \Box \bot \notin \mathcal{V}^e_\Box$.
  \begin{proof}
    (1) Take any $\Diamond$-p model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$,
    then take any $x, x'$ such that $x \leq x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \neg \Box \bot$.
    Then $x' \leq x'$ implies $x' \nVdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \Box \bot$, so there is $x''$ such that $x' \leq x'' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Box \bot$,
    which implies there is no $y''$ such that $x'' \sqsubset y''$.
    Now suppose, by way of contradiction, that there is $y'$ such that $x' \sqsubset y'$,
    then $x'' \geq x' \sqsubset y'$, so by the $\Diamond$-p condition, there must be some $y''$ such that $x'' \sqsubset y'' \geq y'$, which is a contradiction.
    Now that there is no $y'$ such that $x' \sqsubset y'$, we obtain $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Box \bot$.
    Therefore, $x \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \neg \Box \bot \to \Box \bot$.

    (2) Consider the model $\mathfrak{M} = (W, \leq, \sqsubset, V)$, where $W = \set{ x, x', x'', y, y' }$,
    $\mathord{\leq}$ is the reflexive and transitive closure of $\set{ (x, x'), (x', x''), (y, y') }$,
    $\mathord{\sqsubset} = \set{ (x, y), (x', y')}$,
    and $V$ is arbitrary.
    The model $\mathfrak{M}$ is visualized in Figure (fig:nnb-countermodel of the paper).

    \begin{figure}[ht]
      \centering
      \begin{tikzpicture}
      \fill (0, 0) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 0) node[left]{$x$};

      \fill (2, 0) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2, 0) node[right]{$y$};

      \fill (0, 2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 2) node[left]{$x'$};

      \fill (2, 2) circle (2pt);
      \draw (2, 2) node[right]{$y' \nVdash \bot$};

      \fill (0, 4) circle (2pt);
      \draw (0, 4) node[right]{$x'' \Vdash \Box \bot$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0,0) to (0,2);
      \draw (0,1) node[left]{$\leq$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel, squig] (0,0) to (2,0);
      \draw (1, 0) node[below]{$\sqsubset$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (2,0) to (2,2);
      \draw (2,1) node[right]{$\leq$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel, squig] (0,2) to (2,2);
      \draw (1, 2) node[above]{$\sqsubset$};

      \draw [thick, ->, rel] (0,2) to (0,4);
      \draw (0,3) node[left]{$\leq$};

      \end{tikzpicture}
      \caption{The model $\mathfrak{M}$.}

    \end{figure}

    It is easy to see that $\mathfrak{M}$ is a $\Box$-p model.
    Also, we can easily check that $x' \Vdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \neg \Box \bot$,
    $x' \nVdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \Box \bot$,
    and so $x \nVdash^e_\mathfrak{M} \neg \neg \Box \bot \to \Box \bot$.
  \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition d:trigonometric polynomials of arXiv:2605.22803, 'Persistence of asymptotic variance under transport:\\ from hyperfluctuation to stealthy hy'] A function $p:\R\to\C$ is called a \textit{trigonometric polynomial} of degree $m\in\N_0$ if there are $a_{-m},...,a_m\in\C$ such that $a_{-m}\neq 0$ or $a_m\neq 0$, and
    \begin{equation}
        p(x) = \sum_{k=-m}^m a_k e^{ikx},\quad x\in\R.
    \end{equation}
    Similarly, a function $p:\R^l\to\C$ is called a \textit{multivariate trigonometric polynomial} of degree $(m_1,...,m_l)\in\N_0^l$ if there are $a_{k_1,...,k_l}\in\C$, $k_1,...,k_l\in\Z$ with $|k_j|\leq m_j$ for $j\in\{1,...,l\}$, such that
    \begin{equation}
        p(x_1,...,x_l) = \sum_{\substack{k_1,...,k_l\in\Z,\\|k_j|\leq m_j \textnormal{ for } j\in\{1,...,l\}}} a_{k_1,...,k_l} e^{ik_1x_1+...+ik_lx_l},\quad x\in\R^l,
    \end{equation}
     and such that, for all $j\in\{1,...,l\}$, there are $k_1,...,k_l\in\Z$ with $a_{k_1,...,k_l}\neq 0$ and $|k_j|=m_j$.

[Lemma cover_to_green of arXiv:2608.00399, 'The Heavy-tailed Frog Model'] Let $A, B \subseteq \mathbb Z^d$ and $\ell \in \mathbb N^+$, then we have 
\begin{align*}
	\sum_{y \in B} \mathbf P_y(\tau_{A} \le \ell) \ge \frac{G_{\ell}(A, B)}{\max_{x \in A} G_{\ell}(x, A)}.
\end{align*}
Similarly, for any $\ell_1, \ell_2 \in \mathbb N^+$ we also have 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{y \in B} \mathbf P_y(\tau_{A} \le \ell_1) \le \frac{G_{\ell_1 + \ell_2}(A, B)}{\max_{x \in A} G_{\ell_2}(x, A)}.
\end{align*}
Moreover, the same inequalities hold on the torus $\mathbb{T}^d_L$ by replacing $G_\ell$ with $G^{(L)}_\ell$ and $\mathbf P_y$ with $\mathbf P^{(L)}_y$ for any $L \in \mathbb N^+$.

