{"artifact_digest":"sha256:e34a95b35454c517ec8d34197633003186f6582f94c3bc8fedea05c5776f21a2","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/e3/e34a95b35454c517ec8d34197633003186f6582f94c3bc8fedea05c5776f21a2.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:8263164b0e27424459ba0b48cf06a2a6e030693f6f3c9f4ac2fddf663b62f071","bundle_entry_key":"2605-26048-lemma-16-buse-w","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_026","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_026.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2605-26048-lemma-16-buse-w","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: By assumption, $g_{(x,t)}^{b,L} = g_{(x,t)}^{\xi,L}$ and $g_{(y,s)}^{b,L} = g_{(y,s)}^{\xi,L}$ have the same color $\xi$, so they coalesce by Lemma (lem:all_coalesce of the paper). Let $p \in \mathbb{R}^2$ be a common point along both geodesics. Then, since $b$ is an eternal solution and the locations of geodesics are maximizers by definition, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        b(x,t) = b(p) + \mathcal{L}(p;x,t),\quad\text{and}\quad b(y,s) = b(p) + \mathcal{L}(p;y,s).
    \end{align*}
