{"artifact_digest":"sha256:40945b83b39cb8d72a7422818915ad065392465e56865fe5b596866a22270f50","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/40/40945b83b39cb8d72a7422818915ad065392465e56865fe5b596866a22270f50.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:64f98484725253eb2b2ea385c1fdc387032fbecefcae18ee593b2b610d133a86","bundle_entry_key":"2509-10984-lemma-31-lpconv","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_091","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_091.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2509-10984-lemma-31-lpconv","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The proof is similar to the proof of [Barnes24]. 
	First, we prove the $L^1$ convergence. Let $T_0>0$ be such that Proposition~(prop:duality of the paper) holds for $X^{n}$, for all $n \geq 0$. Note that
	it is possible to choose $T_0>0$ uniformly in $n$, since it only depends on $\sup_n\nu_n^1(0,\infty)+\nu_n^2(0,\infty)$, where $\nu_n^1, \nu_n^2$ are given by
	\begin{equation*}
