body:
[Cor 21 of arXiv:2507.08909, 'Annealed almost periodic entropy'] The map $\pi^?$ is a surjection from $\rmM_{(k+\ell+m)+}$ onto $\PPSD_{k,\ell,m}$.  Its restriction to $\rmM^\circ_{(k+\ell+m)+}$ is surjective onto $\PPSD^\circ_{k,\ell,m}$.

	We can define a map
	\begin{equation}
		\PPSD_{k,\ell,m}\times \Xi(m,k) \to \rmM_{(k+\ell+m)+}
	\end{equation}
	 according to
	\[(Q^?,C) \mapsto \left[\begin{array}{ccc}Q_{11} & Q_{12} & R\\ Q_{12}^\ast & Q_{22} & Q_{23}\\ R^\ast & Q_{23}^\ast & Q_{33}\end{array}\right],\]
	where $S_{11}$, $S_{22}$ are given by~\eqref{eq:Schurs} and then $R$ is given by~\eqref{eq:param}.  For each $Q^? \in \PPSD_{k,\ell,m}$, this map sends $\{Q^?\}\times \Xi(m,k)$ onto the fibre $\Delta(Q^?)$, and the restriction of this map to $\PPSD^\circ_{k,\ell,m}\times \Xi^\circ(m,k)$ is a homeomorphism onto $\rmM^\circ_{(k+\ell+m)+}$.

[Lem lem-cell-sdp-lower of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] Recall the definition of the lower-right boundary of $M_{0,n}$ from Definition~(def-kmsw of the paper), and recall that $M_{0,n}$ contains all of the edges on its lower-right boundary. 
For each $\delta > 0$, there exists $A = A(\delta) > 1$ 
such that for each $n \in \mathbbm N$, the following is true. With probability at least $1-\delta$, there exists a vertex $y$ on the lower-right boundary of $M_{0,n}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
\operatorname{SDP}_{M_{0,n}}(x_0 , y) \geq A^{-1} n^{3/8}   .
\end{equation*}

[Definition 25 of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] A measurable function \(f:(0,+\infty)\mapsto (0,+\infty)\) is said to be

\begin{itemize}
\item
  \emph{slowly varying} at \(+\infty\) when

  \begin{equation*}{
  \forall \lambda>0,\ \frac{f(\lambda x)}{f(x)} \xrightarrow[x \to \infty]{} 1;
  }\end{equation*}
\item
  \emph{regularly varying with index \(\beta \in \R\)} (or
  \emph{\(\beta\)-varying}) at \(+\infty\) when

  \begin{equation*}{
  \forall \lambda>0,\ \frac{f(\lambda x)}{f(x)} \xrightarrow[x \to \infty]{} \lambda^{\beta},
  }\end{equation*}

  or equivalently when \(x \mapsto x^{-\beta}f(x)\) is a slowly varying
  function at \(+\infty\).
\end{itemize}

[Definition def: presymplectic Hamiltonian action of arXiv:2608.28278, 'Reduction of symmetric time-dependent Hamiltonian systems I: presymplectic principal $\R$-'] A \textit{presymplectic Hamiltonian action}  on a presymplectic manifold $(M, $ $\omega)$ is an action $\Phi: G\times M \to M$ of a Lie group $G$ on $M$, satisfying the following properties: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] The action $\Phi$ is presymplectic, i.e., $\Phi_g^*\omega = \omega$, for all $g\in G$.
    \item[(ii)] For all $x\in M$,
    \begin{equation}
        \ker \omega (x) \,\cap\, T_x(G\cdot x) = \{ 0 \}.
    \end{equation}
    \item[(iii)] For every $\xi \in \mathfrak{g}$, the infinitesimal generator $\xi_{M}$ of $\Phi$ is a Hamiltonian vector field with respect to $\omega$, i.e., there exists a smooth map $J:M \to \mathfrak{g}^*$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        i_{\xi_M}\omega = dJ_\xi,
    \end{equation}
    where the function $J_\xi: M\to \mathbb{R}$ is given by $J_\xi(x) = J(x)(\xi)$ for all $x\in M$. The function $J$ is called the \textit{momentum map} for $\Phi$ on $(M,\omega)$.

\end{itemize}
We call $(M,\omega, \Phi, J)$ a symmetric presymplectic Hamiltonian system.

