body:
[Theorem 1 (Moderate Deviation Principle) of arXiv:2605.08629, 'Moderate deviations for the Maki--Thompson rumour model'] Assume \eqref{bn-condition}, then the sequence $\{Z_n\}_{n\ge 1}$ satisfies a moderate deviation principle with speed $b_n^2$ and rate function
    \begin{equation}
        J(x) = \frac{x^2}{2\sigma^2}, \quad x\in\mathbb{R},
    \end{equation}
    where $\sigma^2$ is given by \eqref{eq-sigma2}. That is, for every Borel set $B\subset\mathbb{R}$,
    \begin{align}
        -\inf_{x\in B^\circ} J(x) &\le \liminf_{n\to\infty} \frac{1}{b_n^2}\log\mathbb{P}(Z_n \in B) \notag \\
        &\le \limsup_{n\to\infty} \frac{1}{b_n^2}\log\mathbb{P}(Z_n \in B)
        \le -\inf_{x\in \overline{B}} J(x),
    \end{align}
    where $B^\circ$ and $\overline{B}$ denote the interior and closure of $B$, respectively. In particular, for any $z>0$,
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{n\to\infty} \frac{1}{b_n^2}\log\mathbb{P}\bigl(|Z_n| \ge z\bigr) = -\frac{z^2}{2\sigma^2}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2606.30364, 'Distributional comparison for non-commutative infinitely divisible probability measures'] If $\Poiss_1$ and $\Poiss_2$ are finite measures on $\R$ with the same total mass $\Poiss_1[\R]=\Poiss_2[\R]$ and finite first moments $m_1[\Poiss_1]$ and $m_1[\Poiss_2]$, then 

\begin{align}
     \freeWass{1} ( \pi_{\Poiss_1}  , \pi_{\Poiss_2}  )  \leq \classWass{1} ( \Poiss_1 , \Poiss_2 )
     +|m_1[\Poiss_1]-m_1[\Poiss_2]|
\end{align}

with $\freeWass{1}$ and $\classWass{1}$ the free and classical $1$-Wasserstein distance, respectively.

