[Thm t.generalgmc of arXiv:2606.11043, 'Thick points under Gaussian free field dynamics'] Let $h$ be a Gaussian, such that the following hypotheses are fulfiled 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(H1)] The correlations of $(h_{\varepsilon}(x))_{\varepsilon\in(0,1), x\in D}$ fulfil
        \begin{equation}
            \mathrm{cov}(h_{\varepsilon}(x),h_{\delta}(y)) = -\ln\left(d(x,y)\lor \varepsilon\lor \delta \right) + O(1),
        \end{equation}
        uniformly for $x,y\in D$ and $\varepsilon, \delta \in [0,1]$.
        \item[(H2)] There is a $\rho>0$ such that
        \begin{equation*}
            \int\int_{\overline{D\times D}}\frac{\sigma(dx)\sigma(dy)}{d(x,y)^{\rho- \varepsilon}} < \infty,
        \end{equation*}
        for every $\varepsilon >0$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then for every $\gamma^2 < 2\rho$, the sequence $\mu_{\varepsilon}^{\gamma}$ defined in (equation eq.generalGMC of the paper) converges in probability to a non-trivial measure $\mu^\gamma$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

