[Proposition prop: martingale property of multiple integrals of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
The process $({\ensuremath{\mathbf X}} ^{(a)}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}}))_{a\in (0,1]}$ is a time-reversed càdlàg martingale with respect to the filtration ${\ensuremath{\mathcal F}}  = ({\ensuremath{\mathcal F}} ^{(a)})_{a\in (0,1]}$.
Furthermore it is bounded in $\mathds{L}^p$ for every $p \in (1,\gamma)$ and, for every $a\in (0,1]$ we have,
\begin{equation}
	\|{\ensuremath{\mathbf X}} ^{(a)}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}})\|_{p} \, \leqslant\, (C_0)^k \|\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}}\|_{q},
\end{equation}
where the constant $C_0 = C_0(p,q,\gamma,\Omega)$ is given by Theorem (thm: Lp estimates for discrete chaos of the paper).
In particular, we have the following limit \({\ensuremath{\mathbf X}} (\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}}):= \lim_{a\downarrow 0} {\ensuremath{\mathbf X}} ^{(a)}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}})\) \({\ensuremath{\mathbb P}} \)-a.s.\ and in \(\mathds{L}^p\).

