body:
[Proposition uniform case convergences of arXiv:2603.09008, 'On the statistics of random-to-top shuffles'] Let $\pi$ be a uniformly random permutation of $[n]$. Let $F(\pi)$ be the number of fixed points, $d(\pi)$ be the number of descents, and $I(\pi)$ be the number of inversions. Then, we have
    \begin{equation} 

    F(\pi)\Rightarrow_d\; \operatorname{Poisson}(1),
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation}

        \frac{d(\pi)-n/2}{\sqrt{n}}\Rightarrow_d\; \mathcal{N}(0,1/12),\;\;\;\; \text{and}
    \end{equation}
    \begin{equation}

        \frac{I(\pi)-n^2 / 4}{n^{3/2}}\Rightarrow_d\; \mathcal{N}(0,1/36).
    \end{equation}

