[Lemm 22 of arXiv:2607.13849, 'Rearranged Stochastic Heat Equation with an Entropy Gradient Structure'] Assume that $\psi_t$ is piecewise constant as described in Remark \ref{remark:4.10}. Then the integral process $\Big(\int_0^t \psi_r\cdot \mathrm{d}\mathscr{J}_r\Big)_{t\in [0,T]}$ as given in Definition \ref{defi114}, coincides with the standard Riemann sums 
\begin{align*}
\int_0^t \psi_r\cdot \mathrm{d}\mathscr{J}_r = \lim_{\mathrm{mesh}(\pi_n)\searrow 0} \sum_{t_i, t_{t+1}\in \pi_n}\langle \psi_{t_i},\mathscr{J}_{t_{i+1}\wedge t}-\mathscr{J}_{t_i} \rangle.
\end{align*}
In particular, if for each $i\geq 0$, $\psi_{t_i}$ is symmetric non-decreasing, then
\begin{align*}
\int_0^t \psi_r\cdot \mathrm{d}\mathscr{J}_r\geq \frac{1}{2}\int_0^t\int_{\mathbb{S}}\frac{\psi_{r}'}{\varepsilon(\mathbf{x})+ D{X}_{r}(\mathbf{x})}\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}\mathrm{d}r\geq 0,
\end{align*}
where $\varepsilon(\mathbf{x})$ is given by \eqref{eq:varepsilon}.

