{"artifact_digest":"sha256:a8c092c0e34d7971d71ca35849c3d32f83d4281836e762191615866c56550e9e","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/a8/a8c092c0e34d7971d71ca35849c3d32f83d4281836e762191615866c56550e9e.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:a6610ba175fb01a640032e41ba49893d2b957242e8e783b165d17a78cd44373d","bundle_entry_key":"2604-13454-lemma-36-lemmaapproximatelycontrolable","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_013","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_013.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2604-13454-lemma-36-lemmaapproximatelycontrolable","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: For simplicity, we only prove the three-dimensional case. For arbitrary $T> 0$, $x=(x_1 , x_2 , x_3)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^3$, $z=(z_1 , z_2 , z_3)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^3$ and $u=(u_1 , u_2, u_3)^\top \in L^2(0,T;\mathbb{R}^3)$ , we can write $y(T , x;u)$ as
