body:
[Lemma homotopy-po-equiv-fixed-points of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] A commutative square 
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{tikzcd}
            A\arrow[r]\arrow[d] & B\arrow[d]\\
            C\arrow[r] & D
        \end{tikzcd}
    \end{equation}
    in $\textbf{\textup{$\bm G$-Top}}$ is a homotopy pushout in the ${\mathcal{F}}$-model structure if and only if for every $H\in{\mathcal{F}}$ the resulting square on $H$-fixed points is a homotopy pushout in $\textbf{\textup{Top}}$.
    \begin{proof}
        Pick a factorization of $A\to B$ into a cofibration $i$ followed by an ${\mathcal{F}}$-weak equivalence $f$ as depicted in the following diagram:
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                A\arrow[r,"i"]\arrow[d]\arrow[dr,phantom,"\ulcorner"{very near end}] & B_0\arrow[r,"f","\sim"']\arrow[d] & B\arrow[d]\\
                C\arrow[r] & C_0\arrow[r,dashed,"g"'] & D\rlap.
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        By definition, $((diag:orig-square of the paper))$ is a homotopy pushout if and only if $g$ is an ${\mathcal{F}}$-weak equivalence. As $i$ is in particular an h-cofibration, so is $i^H$ for every $H\in{\mathcal{F}}$ by Remark~(rk:fixed-and-restr-preserve-h-cof of the paper); moreover, [schwede2018global]*{Proposition~B.1(i)} shows that if we apply $H$-fixed points to the left-hand square in the above diagram, this is still an (honest) pushout square. Thus, applying the previous lemma for $G=1$, we see that $g^H$ is a weak homotopy equivalence if and only if $((diag:orig-square of the paper))$ induces a homotopy pushout on $H$-fixed points. Letting $H$ vary, this yields precisely the claim.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

