{"artifact_digest":"sha256:93f7870a8158e67f1677ab839f81dcc36b95e14c5e9143593b214d2327ef4bd5","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/93/93f7870a8158e67f1677ab839f81dcc36b95e14c5e9143593b214d2327ef4bd5.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:1f0ef796797b81a7feaae55cc25bd2b399b067ddc5b4fb093c4c2a289384859c","bundle_entry_key":"2604-22644-theorem-5-expected-time","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_019","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_019.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2604-22644-corollary-5-expected-time","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $A(q) = E[q^{\theta_1} I(e_1 \text{ does not reach } N)]$ and $B(q) = E[q^{\tau_N} I(e_1 \text{ reaches } N)]$. From Theorem \ref{thm4}, we have $E[q^{\tau_N}] = \frac{B(q)}{1 - A(q)}$. Differentiating with respect to $q$ yields:
\begin{align}
    \frac{d}{dq} E[q^{\tau_N}] = \frac{B'(q)(1 - A(q)) + B(q)A'(q)}{(1 - A(q))^2}.
\end{align}
Taking the limit as $q \nearrow 1$, we have $A(1) = P(e_1 \text{ does not reach } N)$ and $B(1) = P(e_1 \text{ reaches } N)$. Therefore, by Theorem \ref{thm1}, $1 - A(1) = B(1) = \int_0^1 \frac{p}{S(N;p)} dp$. Substituting these into the derivative expression gives:
