body:
[Definition D:APM of arXiv:2609.03939, 'Arithmetic probability measures'] A measure $\mu \in \mathcal{P}(K)$ is called an \emph{arithmetic probability measure}
if there exists a sequence $\{\alpha_n\}$ of
algebraic {\bf integers} satisfying the following conditions:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item  $\deg \alpha_n \to \infty$ as $n \to \infty$;
\item  For every neighborhood $U$ of $K$, there exists $N \in \mathbb{N}$ such that
$\mathcal O(\alpha_n)\subset U$ for all $n\geq N$;
\item  The normalized counting measure on $\mathcal O(\alpha_n)$ converges to $\mu$ 
in the weak* topology as $n \to \infty$, i.e.,
for every bounded continuous function $f: \mathbb{C} \to \mathbb{R}$,
\[
\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{\deg \alpha_n} \sum_{\gamma \in \mathcal O(\alpha_n)} f(\gamma)= \int f(z) \, d \mu (z).
\]
\end{enumerate}

[Proposition 12 (Existence of $\varsigma_{\alpha,\lambda,c}$ i.e. positivity computation of the function $\varsigma_{\alpha,\lambda,c}^2$ solution of the stabilizer equation for $\alpha \in (1,\frac32)$) of arXiv:2608.31099, '\bf On (fake) Stationarity in Stochastic Volterra Equations with Affine Drift and Regular '] Let $\alpha \in (1,\frac32)$ and consider the volterra equation of the first kind, 
		\begin{equation}
			\kappa \, \left( 1 - R_{\alpha}^2(t) \right) = (f_{\alpha}^2 * g_{\alpha})(t), \quad \forall t \geq 0, \quad \kappa > 0.
		\end{equation}
		with \( R_{\alpha} : \mathbb{R}^+ \to \mathbb{R} \), \( f_{\alpha} := -R_{\alpha}' \) satisfy \( R_{\alpha}(0) = 1 \), \( \lim_{t\to +\infty}R_{\alpha} (t) = 0 \), and \( f_{\alpha}(0)=0 \), \( \lim_{t\to +\infty}f_{\alpha} (t) = 0\)
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item[(a)] Then equation ~\eqref{eq:stabil2} has at most one solution in \( {\cal L}^1_{\text{loc}}(\R_+) \) that converges to a finite limit.

			\item[(b)] If the equation ~\eqref{eq:stabil2} has a continuous solution \( g_{\alpha} \) defined on \( I \subseteq (0, +\infty) \) , then \( g_{\alpha} \geq 0 \) on \( I \subseteq \mathbb{R}^+ \), so that the function \( \sqrt{g_{\alpha}} \) is well-defined on \(I \subseteq \mathbb{R}_+ \).
		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Conjecture of arXiv:2504.18739, 'discrete analogues of second-order Riesz transforms'] \begin{equation}
