[Thm 6 of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] Assume that $(X,\mathbb{P}_x)$ admits transition densities $p_u(\cdot)$ for $u>0$. For $x\in \R$, we then have
\begin{align}
  \Q_x^\mu=\frac{1}{\varphi^\mu(x)}\int_0^\infty \Big((\Gamma_u^\mu\cdot\P_{x,0}^u)\bullet (\Gamma_\infty^\mu\cdot\P_0^{(0)})\Big)p_u(-x)du+\frac{h(x)\cdot \Gamma_\infty^\mu}{\varphi^\mu(x)}\cdot\P_x^{(0)},
\end{align}
where $\mathbb{P}_{x,0}^u$ denotes the \Levy\ bridge from $X_0=x$ to $X_u=0$, $\mathbb{P}_x^{(0)}$ denotes the law of the \Levy\ process conditioned to avoid zero, and the symbol $\bullet$ denotes concatenation. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Defn eq:g of arXiv:2606.02340, 'Transitivity in Inhomogeneous Random Tournaments'] Given a tournamenton $W$, the 1-point conditional homomorphism density function for the circular triad (oriented either clockwise or anticlockwise) is defined as: For $x \in [0, 1]$, 
\begin{align}
    t^{\triangle}_{W}(x) &= \mathbb{E}\left[ W(\eta_1, \eta_2) W(\eta_2, \eta_3) W(\eta_3, \eta_1) \mid \eta_{1}=x \right].     \nonumber \\ 
&=\int_{[0,1]^2}W(x,y)W(y,z)W(z,x)\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}z .

\end{align} 
The tournamenton $W$ is said to be $\triangle$-{\it regular}, if the function $t^{\triangle}_{W}(x)$ is constant for almost every $x \in [0,\,1].$ (Note that if $t^{\triangle}_{W}(x)$ is constant almost everywhere, by integrating both sides of (equation eq:g of the paper) it follows that the constant must be $t(\cc, W)$.)

