[Lemma of arXiv:2603.14302, 'On Partition Functions for Time-Inhomogeneous Branching Random Walks'] (Upper bounds)
        Let $(a_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence of real numbers such that $|a_n| \to \infty$ as $n \to \infty$. Suppose that for every $\alpha \in (0,1)$, we have:
        \begin{equation*}
            \mathbb{E}[(\bar{W}_n^{\beta_c})^{\alpha}] = e^{\alpha a_n(1+ o(1))}.
        \end{equation*}
        Then $\bar{W}_n^{\beta_c} \leq e^{a_n (1 + o(1))}$ in probability as $n \to \infty$. For our purposes, we set $a_n=-\frac{1}{2}\log n$. Moreover, if for any $\epsilon > 0$, $\sum_{n \geq 1} e^{- \epsilon |a_n|} < \infty$, then $\bar{W}_n^{\beta_c}\leq e^{a_n (1 + o(1))}$ almost surely.

