body:
[Theorem thm:main-poc of arXiv:2607.19583, 'Density-Dependent McKean--Vlasov Diffusions: Subgaussian Occupancy Bounds and Polynomial P'] Under Assumptions~(ass:coefficients of the paper) and (ass:density of the paper), there exists a constant $C_T<\infty$, independent of $N\ge2$, $0<h\le1$, $L$, and the shift locations, such that for $0\le t\le T$:
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Ent}\!\left(P_t^N\mid p_t^{\otimes N}\right) \le C_T\bigl(Nh^2(1+|\log h|)+h^{-d}+\log N\bigr).

\end{equation}
Consequently, for any $1\le k\le N$, the marginal entropy and total variation distance satisfy:
\begin{align}
 \operatorname{Ent}\!\left(P_t^{N,k}\mid p_t^{\otimes k}\right) &\le C_T k\left(h^2(1+|\log h|)+\frac{h^{-d}+\log N}{N}\right), \\
 \|P_t^{N,k}-p_t^{\otimes k}\|_{\mathrm{TV}} &\le C_T\sqrt{k} \left(h\sqrt{1+|\log h|}+\sqrt{\frac{h^{-d}+\log N}{N}}\right). 
\end{align}
Furthermore, if $b_i^N$ is the empirical drift in (equation eq:particle-system of the paper) and $\bar b_t(x)=-\Xi(p_t(x))\nabla\Phi(x)$, the time-integrated expected drift error obeys
\begin{equation}
 \int_0^T\mathbb E_{P_t^N}|b_i^N(X_t)-\bar b_t(X_t^{i,N})|^2\,\mathrm d t \le C_T\left[h^2(1+|\log h|)+\frac{h^{-d}+\log N}{N}\right].

\end{equation}
The constant $C_T$ tracks the application of Gronwall's inequality and may grow exponentially like $e^{C T}$ or $e^{C T}$ times a polynomial, depending on the parameters $T$, $d$, $C_\Phi$, $\kappa$, $K$, $L_\Xi$, $C_0$, $c_0$, $C_1$, $a_1$, and $m$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition Global-solution-definition-SES of arXiv:2605.16963, 'Stochastic Euler Equations with Pseudo-differential Noise: Continuous and Discontinuous Pe'] An $\{\mathcal{F}_t\}$-progressively measurable $\mathbf{H}$-valued process $X=(X(t))_{t\ge0}$ with càdlàg paths in $\widetilde{\mathbf{H}}$ is called a global solution to (equation SES of the paper) with initial data $X_0 \in \mathbf{H}$ if the following conditions hold $\mathbb{P}$-$\mathrm{a.s.}$:
		\begin{enumerate}[label={\rm(\arabic*)},leftmargin=0.79cm]\setlength\itemsep{0.2em}
			\item $\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\|X(t)\|_{\mathbf{H}}<\infty$ for every $T>0$;
			\item the integral equation (equation SES of the paper) holds identically for all $t\ge0$ as an equality in $\widetilde{\mathbf{H}}$.
		\end{enumerate}

[Lemma l5.3 of arXiv:2411.19255, 'Moderate, large and super large deviations principles for Poisson process with uniform cat'] The family of random variables $\frac{1}{\varphi(T)}\big(\nu_1(T)-\nu_1(T-c\varphi(T))\big)$ satisfied LDP with
normalized function $\psi(T)=\varphi(T)$ and with rate function
$$
\tilde{I}(x)= \left\{
           \begin{array}{lcl}
                              \ \infty,  \text{ if }\ x\in (-\infty,0),\\
                              x\ln\left(\frac{x(\lambda+\mu)}{\alpha\lambda c}\right)-x+\frac{\alpha\lambda c}{\lambda+\mu}, \text{ if }\ x\in [0,\infty).\\
                              \end{array}
                              \right.
$$

[Lemma lemma-convergence2PTS of arXiv:2507.19222, 'Convergence of the KMP model to the KPZ equation'] For all $x_{1},x_{2},y_{1},y_{2}\in \mathbb{Z}$ we have that
	\begin{equation}
		\lim_{\epsilon\to 0} \mathbf{p}_{\lfloor\epsilon^{-1}\rfloor}^{(2),\epsilon}\left((x_{1},x_{2})\to (y_{1},y_{2})\right)=\mathbf{p}^{(2)}\left((x_{1},x_{2})\to (y_{1},y_{2})\right)\,.
	\end{equation}
	Moreover, for all $x\in \mathbb{Z}$, we have that 
	\begin{equation}
		\lim_{\epsilon\to 0}\mathbf{p}_{\lfloor\epsilon^{-1}\rfloor}^{\epsilon}(x)=\mathbf{p}(x)\,.
	\end{equation}

