[Definition def:AT metric of arXiv:2411.11345, 'Real Gaussian exponential sums via a real moment map'] We say that the Gaussian random field $\mathcal{X}$ is \emph{non degenerate} if $\zeta_\mathcal{X}(x)$ is full dimensional for all $x\in M$. In that case, we define the \emph{Adler-Taylor} (AT) metric on $M$ to be the Riemannian metric such that for all $x\in M$, its unit ball $B_x\subset T_xM$ satisfy 
  \begin{equation}
    \zeta_\mathcal{X}(x)=\frac{1}{2\pi}B_x^\circ.
  \end{equation}

[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2609.01540, '2609.01540'] The functional \eqref{eq:2.12} attains its minimum on \(\mathcal{A}_Y\). Every
minimizer has a smooth representative \(u_p\) and

