[Lemma 15 of arXiv:2605.30055, 'The Wasserstein cost of importance sampling'] For any $\varepsilon > 0$ and any $Q \subset (0,1)^d$, there is a constant $C(\varepsilon)$ such that
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{P}\left(\gamma > (1 + \varepsilon)\frac{\int_Q g}{\int_Q f}\right) \leqslant 2e^{-n|Q|C(\varepsilon)}.
\end{equation}

