[Definition de:solution of arXiv:2411.12192, 'Sample path properties and small ball probabilities for stochastic fractional diffusion eq'] A jointly measurable process $\{u(t,x);(t,x)\in[0,T]\times\mathbb{R}^d\}$ is said to be a solution of the stochastic Cauchy problem
  (equation e:fde of the paper), if for any $\varphi(t,x)\in\mathcal{D}\big((0,T)\times\mathbb{R}^d\big)$,
  \begin{equation}
    \int_{0}^{T}\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} u(t,x)\varphi(t,x)\ensuremath{ \mathrm{d}}  t\ensuremath{ \mathrm{d}}  x=  \int_{0}^{T}\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} (\varphi\ast  \tilde{p})(t,x)W(\ensuremath{ \mathrm{d}}  t,\ensuremath{ \mathrm{d}}  x), \quad \text{a.s.}
  \end{equation}
  Here, we recall that $\tilde{p}(t,x):=p(-t,-x)$ and the symbol $\ast$ denotes the convolution.

[Proposition pro.velocity-resolvent-infty of arXiv:2605.26082, 'Quantitative Einstein relation for reversible diffusions in a random environment'] There exists $C<\infty$ such that, for $\mathbb{Q}$-a.e.\ $\omega$ and every $(m,h,\rho)$ in the admissible regime~(equation eq.admissible of the paper), the velocity resolvent $u_m$ satisfies
		\begin{equation}
			\bigl|3^{-m}u_m(0)-\bar{\mathbf{a}} e_1\bigr|=|U_m(0)|\le C \rho^{1/2} 3^{(d/2+1-\beta_h)h-\delta m}\, .
		\end{equation}

