body:
[Lem lem-uiqbot-boltzmann of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] For $r\geq 1$, let
\begin{equation} 
\widehat\tau_r^R := \min\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft\{ n\in\mathbbm N : \widehat{\mathcal R}(n) = -r \aftergroup\egroup\originalright\}. 
\end{equation}
Let $\widehat M(r)$ be the submap of $\widehat M_{0,\infty}$ obtained by applying the KMSW procedure to $\widehat{\mathcal Z}|_{[0,\widehat\tau_r^R-1]}$. 
Also, let $\widehat z(r)$ be the last (in directed order) vertex on the left boundary of $\widehat M(r)$ which also lies on the left boundary of $\widehat M_{0,\infty}$. 
Then the law of $(\widehat M(r) , \widehat z(r))$ is the Boltzmann distribution on bipolar-oriented triangulations with right boundary length $r$ and a marked left boundary vertex (Definition~(def-boltzmann-right of the paper)). In particular, $\widehat M(r)$ is a bipolar-oriented map with no missing edges. 
Moreover,  with probability tending to one as $A\to\infty$, uniformly in the choice of $r$,
\begin{equation}
    A^{-1}r^{2} \leq \#\mathcal E(\widehat M(r)) \leq Ar^2.
\end{equation}

